const PaperSearchData=[{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 1 · The bridge and its history","aliases":["The fence everyone can see","Christopher Clark"],"work":{"title":"The fence everyone can see","author":"Christopher Clark","description":"An analytical history of Pasadena’s Colorado Street Bridge barrier project, with linked sources."},"text":"Figure 1. The bridge after its 1993 restoration, showing its curve and arches across the Arroyo Seco. BCA Associates. The Colorado Street Bridge has carried two reputations for generations. It’s one of Pasadena’s great landmarks, a 1913 concrete span over the Arroyo Seco that has become part of the city’s identity. It’s also known as “Suicide Bridge.”, Before it opened, crossing the arroyo meant descending into the ravine and climbing out again. The earlier wooden Scoville Bridge crossed the stream below, leaving travelers to negotiate the steep approaches. That was difficult for horse-drawn vehicles and early automobiles. The new bridge carried Colorado Street across the ravine at street level. Engineer John Alexander Low Waddell’s design was adapted by builder John Drake Mercereau. Its sweeping curve helped the builders avoid unstable ground when placing the foundations. Construction began in July 1912. On December 13, 1913, residents celebrated the opening with a parade of decorated automobiles. The bridge later carried Route 66 across the arroyo. An earthquake retrofit and restoration completed in 1993 reconstructed its concrete balustrades and seating alcoves and restored the historic lighting design. The bridge is listed on the National Register of Historic Places, documented by the Historic American Engineering Record, and designated a Pasadena Cultural Heritage Landmark. Pasadena’s Bridge Party, a tradition dating to 1978, celebrates the bridge and the efforts to preserve it., Its big-screen history reaches back to Charlie Chaplin’s The Kid (1921), which used the bridge for a scene in which Edna Purviance’s character considers suicide. Nearly a century later, it appeared in La La Land (2016). The bridge has been a setting for stories, a gathering place, and a landmark people have worked to preserve. But its other history never went away.","route":"paper/","path":"paper/","passages":[{"id":"passage-4","text":"Figure 1. The bridge after its 1993 restoration, showing its curve and arches across the Arroyo Seco. BCA Associates."},{"id":"passage-5","text":"The Colorado Street Bridge has carried two reputations for generations. It’s one of Pasadena’s great landmarks, a 1913 concrete span over the Arroyo Seco that has become part of the city’s identity. It’s also known as “Suicide Bridge.”,"},{"id":"passage-history-crossing","text":"Before it opened, crossing the arroyo meant descending into the ravine and climbing out again. The earlier wooden Scoville Bridge crossed the stream below, leaving travelers to negotiate the steep approaches. That was difficult for horse-drawn vehicles and early automobiles. The new bridge carried Colorado Street across the ravine at street level."},{"id":"passage-history-construction","text":"Engineer John Alexander Low Waddell’s design was adapted by builder John Drake Mercereau. Its sweeping curve helped the builders avoid unstable ground when placing the foundations. Construction began in July 1912. On December 13, 1913, residents celebrated the opening with a parade of decorated automobiles."},{"id":"passage-history-restoration","text":"The bridge later carried Route 66 across the arroyo. An earthquake retrofit and restoration completed in 1993 reconstructed its concrete balustrades and seating alcoves and restored the historic lighting design."},{"id":"passage-6","text":"The bridge is listed on the National Register of Historic Places, documented by the Historic American Engineering Record, and designated a Pasadena Cultural Heritage Landmark. Pasadena’s Bridge Party, a tradition dating to 1978, celebrates the bridge and the efforts to preserve it.,"},{"id":"passage-7","text":"Its big-screen history reaches back to Charlie Chaplin’s The Kid (1921), which used the bridge for a scene in which Edna Purviance’s character considers suicide. Nearly a century later, it appeared in La La Land (2016). The bridge has been a setting for stories, a gathering place, and a landmark people have worked to preserve. But its other history never went away."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 2 · 2017–2018. An emergency response to an old problem","aliases":[],"text":"2017–2018. An emergency response to an old problem By 2017, that other history had become impossible to ignore. The City counted 28 deaths by suicide at the bridge from 2006 through 2016, and staff reported an increase in suicides and attempts. On July 19, staff gave the Public Safety Committee a presentation on deaths at the bridge and possible ways to prevent them., The presentation also put those deaths in a longer context. It reported more than 150 deaths by suicide at the bridge since 1919, including an estimated 79 during the 1930s. It showed fencing and netting designs the City had drawn up around 1932 and said a police detail at the bridge during that decade cost $20,000 a year. Fence proposal · circa 1932 Netting proposal · circa 1932 Figure 2. City engineering proposals reproduced in the 2017 presentation. These drawings show proposed designs, not installed conditions. Even then, a barrier did not come quickly. A Glendale News-Press account said Pasadena moved slowly amid concerns about how safety measures would affect the bridge’s appearance, and the City did not install a 7½-foot mesh fence topped with barbed wire until 1937. Writing in 1992, the Los Angeles Times reported no deaths by suicide from the bridge during the two years after the fence was installed. Deaths later resumed, but the newspaper said no single year had again reached the levels recorded during the 1930s. That fence was later replaced. By 1956, Pasadena had installed an iron barrier that curved toward the sidewalk, and that work removed the bridge’s original concrete railing and seating alcoves. In 1989, the City was planning an earthquake retrofit of the bridge. The Los Angeles Times reported that the project would include reworking the iron barrier to reduce what it described as a “tunnel effect.” The City completed the retrofit in 1993, rebuilding the historic concrete railing and seating alcoves and installing wrought-iron fencing above the railing., Mesh fence · 1937 Inward-curving railing · 1988 Figure 3. The mesh fence topped with barbed wire in 1937 and an enlarged detail of the iron railing in 1988. Los Angeles Public Library / Tavo Olmos, Historic American Engineering Record. That was the bridge the committee was considering in 2017. Staff said the rebuilt concrete railing and safety barrier together stood nearly eight feet high. But the way they fit together created footholds that reduced the effective barrier height to about four feet. Staff also identified all 20 restored alcoves and both ends of the bridge as places where people could climb over or get around the barrier.,, The presentation explained why closing those gaps could save lives. It addressed questions that would keep returning in later meetings about whether a barrier could prevent a death or simply move it elsewhere. Interrupting an attempt, it explained, could give an immediate suicidal crisis time to ease. Someone stopped at one bridge would not necessarily die by suicide elsewhere.,,, According to the official minutes of the July 19 meeting, Mayor Terry Tornek supported emergency fencing at the bridge’s alcoves, recommended continuing it along the bridge’s ends and approaches, and asked staff to work with local architects and engineers on a response that would be effective, cost-effective, and aesthetically pleasing. Vice Mayor John J. Kennedy, who chaired the committee, supported the alcove fencing as an emergency measure and said it would give the City time to find a more permanent solution. Councilmember Tyron Hampton supported planting trees below the bridge, as several public speakers had suggested, along with fencing at its ends and approaches, cameras, and patrols. Pasadena’s response began with those restored alcoves, 20 semicircular seating areas that extended from the walkway and offered places to pause and look out over the Arroyo., The City fenced them first in 2017., Restored seating alcove Temporary alcove fence · 2017 Figure 4. A restored alcove before fencing, and temporary alcove fencing completed in July 2017. City of Pasadena presentation. Photograph dates unstated. The precise alcove locations are unconfirmed. Later in 2017, Public Works Director Ara Maloyan brought together a task force to study what the City should do next. The group included the City’s health officer and public health director, a suicide-prevention specialist from Didi Hirsch Mental Health Services, Police, Fire, architects, engineers, and preservation advocates., The task force considered physical barriers, netting, patrols, cameras, motion sensors, crisis phones, signs, lighting, and changes to landscaping and railings. One suggestion was to plant hedges below the bridge to spell “HOPE.” Some ideas were meant to keep a person from reaching the edge. Others were meant to make an intervention more likely., Some of those broad choices were not new. More than 80 years earlier, Pasadena had considered fencing, netting, and a police presence, and the bridge’s appearance had been a concern then as well., This time, the City moved quickly to put temporary fencing around the alcoves, but choosing a permanent barrier design would take much longer.,, Over Labor Day weekend in 2018, first responders spent 13 hours helping a person return to safety. Afterward, the City Manager issued an emergency purchase order for temporary fencing 10 feet high along the bridge’s full length., Figure 5. Temporary chain-link fencing along both sides of the bridge, as shown in the City’s November 2019 presentation. City of Pasadena. A Police Department table presented to the committee in 2021 recorded 10 deaths in 2017, before the full-length fence, and four in 2018, the year it was installed. In the years that followed, it recorded one in 2019, none in 2020, and one through June 13, 2021. Pasadena Now quoted then-City Manager Steve Mermell in September 2018 as saying that nine people had died at the bridge in 2017. The difference remains unexplained. The available records do not provide a complete year-by-year count for recent years. A barrier can save a life by creating time. An immediate suicidal crisis can be brief, even when a person’s distress has lasted much longer. A barrier can delay or interrupt an attempt, giving the immediate crisis time to ease and first responders time to arrive. A physical barrier begins providing protection before anyone has noticed the crisis or arrived to help.,, The full-length fence changed the bridge’s appearance and restricted access. Lower death counts followed its installation. Its possible benefit was harder to see because deaths it may have prevented do not appear in incident totals. The record that followed would repeatedly separate the safety question from the design question. Years later, the emergency measure remains in place, and the City still calls it temporary, though keeping it is itself a choice.,,","route":"paper/2017-2018/","path":"paper/2017-2018/","passages":[{"id":"passage-8","text":"2017–2018. An emergency response to an old problem"},{"id":"passage-9","text":"By 2017, that other history had become impossible to ignore. The City counted 28 deaths by suicide at the bridge from 2006 through 2016, and staff reported an increase in suicides and attempts. On July 19, staff gave the Public Safety Committee a presentation on deaths at the bridge and possible ways to prevent them.,"},{"id":"passage-10","text":"The presentation also put those deaths in a longer context. It reported more than 150 deaths by suicide at the bridge since 1919, including an estimated 79 during the 1930s. It showed fencing and netting designs the City had drawn up around 1932 and said a police detail at the bridge during that decade cost $20,000 a year."},{"id":"passage-11","text":"Fence proposal · circa 1932"},{"id":"passage-13","text":"Netting proposal · circa 1932"},{"id":"passage-15","text":"Figure 2. City engineering proposals reproduced in the 2017 presentation. These drawings show proposed designs, not installed conditions."},{"id":"passage-16","text":"Even then, a barrier did not come quickly. A Glendale News-Press account said Pasadena moved slowly amid concerns about how safety measures would affect the bridge’s appearance, and the City did not install a 7½-foot mesh fence topped with barbed wire until 1937. Writing in 1992, the Los Angeles Times reported no deaths by suicide from the bridge during the two years after the fence was installed. Deaths later resumed, but the newspaper said no single year had again reached the levels recorded during the 1930s."},{"id":"passage-17","text":"That fence was later replaced. By 1956, Pasadena had installed an iron barrier that curved toward the sidewalk, and that work removed the bridge’s original concrete railing and seating alcoves. In 1989, the City was planning an earthquake retrofit of the bridge. The Los Angeles Times reported that the project would include reworking the iron barrier to reduce what it described as a “tunnel effect.” The City completed the retrofit in 1993, rebuilding the historic concrete railing and seating alcoves and installing wrought-iron fencing above the railing.,"},{"id":"passage-18","text":"Mesh fence · 1937 Inward-curving railing · 1988"},{"id":"passage-20","text":"Figure 3. The mesh fence topped with barbed wire in 1937 and an enlarged detail of the iron railing in 1988. Los Angeles Public Library / Tavo Olmos, Historic American Engineering Record."},{"id":"passage-21","text":"That was the bridge the committee was considering in 2017. Staff said the rebuilt concrete railing and safety barrier together stood nearly eight feet high. But the way they fit together created footholds that reduced the effective barrier height to about four feet. Staff also identified all 20 restored alcoves and both ends of the bridge as places where people could climb over or get around the barrier.,,"},{"id":"passage-22","text":"The presentation explained why closing those gaps could save lives. It addressed questions that would keep returning in later meetings about whether a barrier could prevent a death or simply move it elsewhere. Interrupting an attempt, it explained, could give an immediate suicidal crisis time to ease. Someone stopped at one bridge would not necessarily die by suicide elsewhere.,,,"},{"id":"passage-23","text":"According to the official minutes of the July 19 meeting, Mayor Terry Tornek supported emergency fencing at the bridge’s alcoves, recommended continuing it along the bridge’s ends and approaches, and asked staff to work with local architects and engineers on a response that would be effective, cost-effective, and aesthetically pleasing. Vice Mayor John J. Kennedy, who chaired the committee, supported the alcove fencing as an emergency measure and said it would give the City time to find a more permanent solution. Councilmember Tyron Hampton supported planting trees below the bridge, as several public speakers had suggested, along with fencing at its ends and approaches, cameras, and patrols."},{"id":"passage-24","text":"Pasadena’s response began with those restored alcoves, 20 semicircular seating areas that extended from the walkway and offered places to pause and look out over the Arroyo., The City fenced them first in 2017.,"},{"id":"passage-25","text":"Restored seating alcove Temporary alcove fence · 2017"},{"id":"passage-27","text":"Figure 4. A restored alcove before fencing, and temporary alcove fencing completed in July 2017. City of Pasadena presentation. Photograph dates unstated. The precise alcove locations are unconfirmed."},{"id":"passage-28","text":"Later in 2017, Public Works Director Ara Maloyan brought together a task force to study what the City should do next. The group included the City’s health officer and public health director, a suicide-prevention specialist from Didi Hirsch Mental Health Services, Police, Fire, architects, engineers, and preservation advocates.,"},{"id":"passage-29","text":"The task force considered physical barriers, netting, patrols, cameras, motion sensors, crisis phones, signs, lighting, and changes to landscaping and railings. One suggestion was to plant hedges below the bridge to spell “HOPE.” Some ideas were meant to keep a person from reaching the edge. Others were meant to make an intervention more likely.,"},{"id":"passage-30","text":"Some of those broad choices were not new. More than 80 years earlier, Pasadena had considered fencing, netting, and a police presence, and the bridge’s appearance had been a concern then as well., This time, the City moved quickly to put temporary fencing around the alcoves, but choosing a permanent barrier design would take much longer.,,"},{"id":"passage-31","text":"Over Labor Day weekend in 2018, first responders spent 13 hours helping a person return to safety. Afterward, the City Manager issued an emergency purchase order for temporary fencing 10 feet high along the bridge’s full length.,"},{"id":"passage-33","text":"Figure 5. Temporary chain-link fencing along both sides of the bridge, as shown in the City’s November 2019 presentation. City of Pasadena."},{"id":"passage-34","text":"A Police Department table presented to the committee in 2021 recorded 10 deaths in 2017, before the full-length fence, and four in 2018, the year it was installed. In the years that followed, it recorded one in 2019, none in 2020, and one through June 13, 2021. Pasadena Now quoted then-City Manager Steve Mermell in September 2018 as saying that nine people had died at the bridge in 2017. The difference remains unexplained. The available records do not provide a complete year-by-year count for recent years."},{"id":"passage-35","text":"A barrier can save a life by creating time. An immediate suicidal crisis can be brief, even when a person’s distress has lasted much longer. A barrier can delay or interrupt an attempt, giving the immediate crisis time to ease and first responders time to arrive. A physical barrier begins providing protection before anyone has noticed the crisis or arrived to help.,,"},{"id":"passage-36","text":"The full-length fence changed the bridge’s appearance and restricted access. Lower death counts followed its installation. Its possible benefit was harder to see because deaths it may have prevented do not appear in incident totals. The record that followed would repeatedly separate the safety question from the design question. Years later, the emergency measure remains in place, and the City still calls it temporary, though keeping it is itself a choice.,,"}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 3 · 2018–2019. Pasadena chooses a policy direction","aliases":[],"text":"2018–2019. Pasadena chooses a policy direction Months before the full-length fence went up, Councilmember Steve Madison supported moving forward with the task-force recommendation and asked staff to provide dimensions or a visual of the proposed barrier before the matter reached the full Council, according to the official minutes of the April 18, 2018 Public Safety Committee meeting. Tornek specifically requested a visual showing the scale of the proposed barriers. The task force’s written recommendation set a minimum barrier height of 7 feet 6 inches above the highest foothold, not simply above the walkway. The committee unanimously forwarded the recommendation to the Council. On April 23, Public Works presented the task force’s recommendation to the full City Council. Staff reviewed the alternatives the group had considered, including patrols, technology, netting, landscaping, and vertical barriers. Councilmember Hampton asked staff to consider temporary fencing at the bridge’s ends and approaches until a fence based on an approved design could be constructed. Councilmember Andy Wilson asked whether restricting access at the Colorado Street Bridge would simply push people to another location. Public Health Director Michael Johnson said research on barriers at bridges known for suicide suggested that they can reduce overall deaths rather than simply shift them elsewhere. Councilmember Victor Gordo followed up, asking whether a barrier would reduce suicides across the region or merely shift where they occurred. Johnson said the research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed and referred to a Toronto study. The discussion also briefly turned to the bridge’s transportation role. Councilmember Gene Masuda asked whether pedestrians could simply be prohibited. Maloyan said banning pedestrians from the bridge wouldn’t be enough. Preventing access would also require closing the bridge to vehicle traffic, which he called impossible. When Councilmember Margaret McAustin asked what the design consultant would actually study, Maloyan said the next phase would focus specifically on vertical barriers. The consultant would develop different versions of a barrier and bring them through review by preservation experts, designers, and the public. Tornek predicted that the night’s agreement was probably the highest level of consensus the process would reach. At the end of the discussion, Madison moved the staff recommendation, Hampton seconded it, and the Council unanimously adopted the task force’s direction toward a permanent physical deterrent. The task force’s recommendation centered on a physical barrier designed to keep people from reaching the edge. Other measures could still play a role, but the barrier became the foundation of the City’s approach., The Council had agreed on a barrier, but its design was still unsettled. In May 2018, it set aside $400,000 for environmental and design work. Public Works began seeking a consultant that October and interviewed two firms in February 2019. When the proposed contract reached the Public Safety Committee on April 17, 2019, Tornek objected that the schedule was too slow given the urgency of the issue, according to the official minutes. Madison cautioned that rushing projects had previously produced community and Council opposition, especially when a historic landmark was involved. He nevertheless supported a revised timeline and asked staff to review the proposed budget and pursue grant opportunities. When the committee returned to the contract on May 15, the official minutes record that Madison moved the revised recommendation, Tornek seconded it, and the committee approved it unanimously. At its May 20 meeting, the full Council approved the contract. Public Works had shortened the design schedule by 10 months and reduced the proposed consultant contract from $700,000 to $500,000. At that point, construction was expected to begin around August 2020, provided the City approved additional construction funding. Even with the shorter design schedule, Wilson called it “almost a four or five year project.” Madison had warned against rushing. The years that followed were not a rush.","route":"paper/2018-2019/","path":"paper/2018-2019/","passages":[{"id":"passage-37","text":"2018–2019. Pasadena chooses a policy direction"},{"id":"passage-38","text":"Months before the full-length fence went up, Councilmember Steve Madison supported moving forward with the task-force recommendation and asked staff to provide dimensions or a visual of the proposed barrier before the matter reached the full Council, according to the official minutes of the April 18, 2018 Public Safety Committee meeting. Tornek specifically requested a visual showing the scale of the proposed barriers. The task force’s written recommendation set a minimum barrier height of 7 feet 6 inches above the highest foothold, not simply above the walkway. The committee unanimously forwarded the recommendation to the Council."},{"id":"passage-39","text":"On April 23, Public Works presented the task force’s recommendation to the full City Council. Staff reviewed the alternatives the group had considered, including patrols, technology, netting, landscaping, and vertical barriers."},{"id":"passage-40","text":"Councilmember Hampton asked staff to consider temporary fencing at the bridge’s ends and approaches until a fence based on an approved design could be constructed."},{"id":"passage-41","text":"Councilmember Andy Wilson asked whether restricting access at the Colorado Street Bridge would simply push people to another location. Public Health Director Michael Johnson said research on barriers at bridges known for suicide suggested that they can reduce overall deaths rather than simply shift them elsewhere. Councilmember Victor Gordo followed up, asking whether a barrier would reduce suicides across the region or merely shift where they occurred. Johnson said the research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed and referred to a Toronto study."},{"id":"passage-42","text":"The discussion also briefly turned to the bridge’s transportation role. Councilmember Gene Masuda asked whether pedestrians could simply be prohibited. Maloyan said banning pedestrians from the bridge wouldn’t be enough. Preventing access would also require closing the bridge to vehicle traffic, which he called impossible."},{"id":"passage-43","text":"When Councilmember Margaret McAustin asked what the design consultant would actually study, Maloyan said the next phase would focus specifically on vertical barriers. The consultant would develop different versions of a barrier and bring them through review by preservation experts, designers, and the public."},{"id":"passage-44","text":"Tornek predicted that the night’s agreement was probably the highest level of consensus the process would reach."},{"id":"passage-45","text":"At the end of the discussion, Madison moved the staff recommendation, Hampton seconded it, and the Council unanimously adopted the task force’s direction toward a permanent physical deterrent. The task force’s recommendation centered on a physical barrier designed to keep people from reaching the edge. Other measures could still play a role, but the barrier became the foundation of the City’s approach.,"},{"id":"passage-46","text":"The Council had agreed on a barrier, but its design was still unsettled. In May 2018, it set aside $400,000 for environmental and design work. Public Works began seeking a consultant that October and interviewed two firms in February 2019."},{"id":"passage-47","text":"When the proposed contract reached the Public Safety Committee on April 17, 2019, Tornek objected that the schedule was too slow given the urgency of the issue, according to the official minutes. Madison cautioned that rushing projects had previously produced community and Council opposition, especially when a historic landmark was involved. He nevertheless supported a revised timeline and asked staff to review the proposed budget and pursue grant opportunities."},{"id":"passage-48","text":"When the committee returned to the contract on May 15, the official minutes record that Madison moved the revised recommendation, Tornek seconded it, and the committee approved it unanimously. At its May 20 meeting, the full Council approved the contract. Public Works had shortened the design schedule by 10 months and reduced the proposed consultant contract from $700,000 to $500,000. At that point, construction was expected to begin around August 2020, provided the City approved additional construction funding."},{"id":"passage-49","text":"Even with the shorter design schedule, Wilson called it “almost a four or five year project.” Madison had warned against rushing. The years that followed were not a rush."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 4 · 2021. Selection is deferred","aliases":[],"text":"2021. Selection is deferred By August 2021, more than three years had passed since the City Council’s April 2018 decision to pursue a vertical barrier. The project had involved design work, public outreach, commission review, and full-scale mockups. The committee now had to decide whether to recommend a design, keep the temporary fence in place for the time being, or take another course., Responses to the mockups Option B led among the 1,038 respondents who ranked the mockups. The public survey drew 2,268 responses overall. Design rankings from 1,038 respondents Design Votes Share Curved-curved mesh (Option B) 462 44.5% Pickets (Option C) 360 34.7% Curved-straight mesh (Option A) 216 20.8% Separately, staff reported that 44% of 622 comments preferred no barriers and a return to the bridge’s original state. These were different response groups. Participation was self-selected, so neither result represents Pasadena residents as a whole. After reviewing full-scale mockups, the Historic Preservation Commission voted to recommend Option B with modifications. This was the mesh option the City called “curved-curved.” Its barrier curved inward over the walkway along the entire bridge. Option A curved at the lamp posts and alcoves, with straight sections elsewhere. The Design Commission preferred straight mesh panels between the alcoves and requested further study to improve transparency around the alcoves and light poles. When the issue reached the Public Safety Committee, Pasadena Heritage executive director Sue Mossman said the task force itself still hadn’t agreed on a final design. The designers had explained their reasoning at the March 25 open house. Steve Line of Donald MacDonald Architects described mesh as a way to preserve views while making the barrier difficult to climb. He said straight pickets would require a taller structure and changes to the light standards, and would appear more obstructive when viewed at an angle. Those judgments helped explain the team’s preference for mesh. Community responses differed over both the designs and what should happen next. Pasadena Beautiful urged the City to reconsider horizontal netting. Pasadena Heritage found none of the three mockups acceptable as presented and asked for more compatible options. In June, the West Pasadena Residents’ Association explicitly requested delaying implementation while alternatives received further study. In an August letter, former Pasadena resident Kathleen Clary Miller recalled crossing the bridge as a child and its enduring place in her life. She supported a permanent barrier and hoped its design would preserve the character of a place she cherished. A · Curved-straight mesh B · Curved-curved mesh C · Straight pickets Figure 6. Full-scale mockups from 2021. The red supports were temporary and not part of the proposed designs. City of Pasadena / Donald MacDonald Architects. Mossman had made the preservation problem plain as early as 2018. Keeping people from the edge was only one goal. The harder task was protecting people while preserving the bridge’s historic character and appearance. The August committee discussion At the August 18, 2021 Public Safety Committee meeting, Madison said the temporary fencing had been effective and brought up “coupling,” a concept he recalled from Malcolm Gladwell’s 2019 book Talking to Strangers. Gladwell’s account draws on peer-reviewed suicide-prevention studies. The idea is that a suicidal crisis can become tied to a particular place or method. Making access harder can interrupt an attempt without necessarily moving it somewhere else.,, Researchers use the term “suicide hotspot” for an accessible place where people repeatedly die by suicide and that becomes known for those deaths. A Vancouver study found that some people chose well-known bridges over others closer to home. The researchers suggested that a bridge’s reputation might help explain that pattern. On the design question, Madison was not ready to choose. He proposed narrowing the field to two options and taking them through another round of community input. Kennedy, who had chaired the committee in 2017, wanted to narrow the options and put the project on a timetable. At the same time, he supported going out “one last time” for additional local expertise to help the City get the decision right.,, One option still in play had originated with Kennedy. The February 2020 presentation compared straight mesh, pickets, curved mesh, and a hybrid. It projected mockup installation in March, commission reviews in April, and a committee recommendation and Council approval in May. At the meeting, Kennedy requested a cost estimate for enclosing the bridge with a roof before Council consideration., The City later showed an initial enclosure concept to the task force, including Pasadena Heritage and police and fire representatives. The concept wasn’t accepted because of concerns about appearance and safety. At that August meeting, Steve Line of Donald MacDonald Architects presented refined enclosure concepts to the committee. He said one performed especially well when measured against the team’s three design goals of effectiveness, historic compatibility, and appearance. Hampton said he had thought Kennedy’s original enclosure idea was a strong one, but wanted something that looked less modern. Madison was more skeptical. He questioned whether preservation advocates or the broader public would accept a barrier that fully enclosed the bridge. Kennedy, who had raised the idea, was now ready to move away from it and narrow the field to the vertical-barrier options., When Mayor Victor Gordo, who chaired the Public Safety Committee at the time, spoke later in the meeting, he picked up the idea Madison had raised about place and method. He said the temporary structure appeared to be working and that “the decoupling, if you will, has started.” But he still didn’t think the committee was ready to narrow the permanent designs and called for more options and more public input. Gordo also took up Kennedy’s enclosure concept. He said he had initially thought full enclosure would never work and had pictured something more like a tunnel. After seeing the refined work, however, he said he liked “the idea of pursuing the material,” wondered whether a different material could be used for the arch-like elements, and said the City shouldn’t rule the concept out., Staffing and further review Hampton also said the temporary structure was working and proposed staffing the bridge 24 hours a day. City Manager Steve Mermell said staffing had been suggested before, would likely cost several hundred thousand dollars a year, and wasn’t feasible as a replacement. He recommended leaving the temporary structure in place until a permanent solution was found. Gordo developed the idea using Pasadena’s former Host/Guide program as a model, with trained people carrying radios and serving as additional eyes and ears. He suggested pairing that approach with the City’s ROSE youth-employment program, docents, or tours. Madison pointed to the bridge’s length, curvature, and the speed of some incidents. Kennedy raised liability and intervention concerns. Gordo asked staff to see whether staffing could work alongside a physical barrier. “Get it right” became a refrain during the meeting. Mermell, Kennedy, Gordo, and Hampton used the phrase or variations at least nine times, eight within a span of 25 minutes. Near the end, Mermell summarized the requests for more time and a faster result. He then said three months was probably too aggressive and acknowledged that the process ahead would take longer. Kennedy also wondered whether the pandemic might help explain why no deaths were recorded at the bridge in 2020 and suggested the question was worth studying. Deaths had already fallen to one in 2019, before the pandemic., The committee made no selection. The project went back for more design work, community input, and analysis., John J. Kennedy died on July 21, 2022, while serving on the City Council and only weeks after winning reelection. He had represented District 3 since 2013. Justin Jones was appointed later that year to represent District 3 and would take Kennedy’s place in the bridge discussions that followed. He later won the seat in the March 2024 special election.","route":"paper/2021/","path":"paper/2021/","passages":[{"id":"passage-50","text":"2021. Selection is deferred"},{"id":"passage-51","text":"By August 2021, more than three years had passed since the City Council’s April 2018 decision to pursue a vertical barrier. The project had involved design work, public outreach, commission review, and full-scale mockups. The committee now had to decide whether to recommend a design, keep the temporary fence in place for the time being, or take another course.,"},{"id":"passage-2021-mockup-heading","text":"Responses to the mockups"},{"id":"survey-2021-finding","text":"Option B led among the 1,038 respondents who ranked the mockups. The public survey drew 2,268 responses overall."},{"id":"survey-2021-rankings","text":"Design rankings from 1,038 respondents Design Votes Share Curved-curved mesh (Option B) 462 44.5% Pickets (Option C) 360 34.7% Curved-straight mesh (Option A) 216 20.8%"},{"id":"survey-2021-comments","text":"Separately, staff reported that 44% of 622 comments preferred no barriers and a return to the bridge’s original state. These were different response groups. Participation was self-selected, so neither result represents Pasadena residents as a whole."},{"id":"passage-53","text":"After reviewing full-scale mockups, the Historic Preservation Commission voted to recommend Option B with modifications. This was the mesh option the City called “curved-curved.” Its barrier curved inward over the walkway along the entire bridge. Option A curved at the lamp posts and alcoves, with straight sections elsewhere. The Design Commission preferred straight mesh panels between the alcoves and requested further study to improve transparency around the alcoves and light poles. When the issue reached the Public Safety Committee, Pasadena Heritage executive director Sue Mossman said the task force itself still hadn’t agreed on a final design."},{"id":"passage-2021-open-house","text":"The designers had explained their reasoning at the March 25 open house. Steve Line of Donald MacDonald Architects described mesh as a way to preserve views while making the barrier difficult to climb. He said straight pickets would require a taller structure and changes to the light standards, and would appear more obstructive when viewed at an angle. Those judgments helped explain the team’s preference for mesh."},{"id":"passage-2021-community-positions","text":"Community responses differed over both the designs and what should happen next. Pasadena Beautiful urged the City to reconsider horizontal netting. Pasadena Heritage found none of the three mockups acceptable as presented and asked for more compatible options. In June, the West Pasadena Residents’ Association explicitly requested delaying implementation while alternatives received further study."},{"id":"passage-2021-miller","text":"In an August letter, former Pasadena resident Kathleen Clary Miller recalled crossing the bridge as a child and its enduring place in her life. She supported a permanent barrier and hoped its design would preserve the character of a place she cherished."},{"id":"passage-54","text":"A · Curved-straight mesh B · Curved-curved mesh C · Straight pickets"},{"id":"passage-56","text":"Figure 6. Full-scale mockups from 2021. The red supports were temporary and not part of the proposed designs. City of Pasadena / Donald MacDonald Architects."},{"id":"passage-57","text":"Mossman had made the preservation problem plain as early as 2018. Keeping people from the edge was only one goal. The harder task was protecting people while preserving the bridge’s historic character and appearance."},{"id":"passage-2021-committee-heading","text":"The August committee discussion"},{"id":"passage-58","text":"At the August 18, 2021 Public Safety Committee meeting, Madison said the temporary fencing had been effective and brought up “coupling,” a concept he recalled from Malcolm Gladwell’s 2019 book Talking to Strangers. Gladwell’s account draws on peer-reviewed suicide-prevention studies. The idea is that a suicidal crisis can become tied to a particular place or method. Making access harder can interrupt an attempt without necessarily moving it somewhere else.,,"},{"id":"passage-59","text":"Researchers use the term “suicide hotspot” for an accessible place where people repeatedly die by suicide and that becomes known for those deaths. A Vancouver study found that some people chose well-known bridges over others closer to home. The researchers suggested that a bridge’s reputation might help explain that pattern."},{"id":"passage-60","text":"On the design question, Madison was not ready to choose. He proposed narrowing the field to two options and taking them through another round of community input."},{"id":"passage-61","text":"Kennedy, who had chaired the committee in 2017, wanted to narrow the options and put the project on a timetable. At the same time, he supported going out “one last time” for additional local expertise to help the City get the decision right.,,"},{"id":"passage-62","text":"One option still in play had originated with Kennedy. The February 2020 presentation compared straight mesh, pickets, curved mesh, and a hybrid. It projected mockup installation in March, commission reviews in April, and a committee recommendation and Council approval in May. At the meeting, Kennedy requested a cost estimate for enclosing the bridge with a roof before Council consideration., The City later showed an initial enclosure concept to the task force, including Pasadena Heritage and police and fire representatives. The concept wasn’t accepted because of concerns about appearance and safety."},{"id":"passage-63","text":"At that August meeting, Steve Line of Donald MacDonald Architects presented refined enclosure concepts to the committee. He said one performed especially well when measured against the team’s three design goals of effectiveness, historic compatibility, and appearance."},{"id":"passage-64","text":"Hampton said he had thought Kennedy’s original enclosure idea was a strong one, but wanted something that looked less modern. Madison was more skeptical. He questioned whether preservation advocates or the broader public would accept a barrier that fully enclosed the bridge. Kennedy, who had raised the idea, was now ready to move away from it and narrow the field to the vertical-barrier options.,"},{"id":"passage-65","text":"When Mayor Victor Gordo, who chaired the Public Safety Committee at the time, spoke later in the meeting, he picked up the idea Madison had raised about place and method. He said the temporary structure appeared to be working and that “the decoupling, if you will, has started.” But he still didn’t think the committee was ready to narrow the permanent designs and called for more options and more public input."},{"id":"passage-66","text":"Gordo also took up Kennedy’s enclosure concept. He said he had initially thought full enclosure would never work and had pictured something more like a tunnel. After seeing the refined work, however, he said he liked “the idea of pursuing the material,” wondered whether a different material could be used for the arch-like elements, and said the City shouldn’t rule the concept out.,"},{"id":"passage-2021-staffing-heading","text":"Staffing and further review"},{"id":"passage-67","text":"Hampton also said the temporary structure was working and proposed staffing the bridge 24 hours a day. City Manager Steve Mermell said staffing had been suggested before, would likely cost several hundred thousand dollars a year, and wasn’t feasible as a replacement. He recommended leaving the temporary structure in place until a permanent solution was found."},{"id":"passage-68","text":"Gordo developed the idea using Pasadena’s former Host/Guide program as a model, with trained people carrying radios and serving as additional eyes and ears. He suggested pairing that approach with the City’s ROSE youth-employment program, docents, or tours. Madison pointed to the bridge’s length, curvature, and the speed of some incidents. Kennedy raised liability and intervention concerns. Gordo asked staff to see whether staffing could work alongside a physical barrier."},{"id":"passage-69","text":"“Get it right” became a refrain during the meeting. Mermell, Kennedy, Gordo, and Hampton used the phrase or variations at least nine times, eight within a span of 25 minutes. Near the end, Mermell summarized the requests for more time and a faster result. He then said three months was probably too aggressive and acknowledged that the process ahead would take longer."},{"id":"passage-70","text":"Kennedy also wondered whether the pandemic might help explain why no deaths were recorded at the bridge in 2020 and suggested the question was worth studying. Deaths had already fallen to one in 2019, before the pandemic.,"},{"id":"passage-71","text":"The committee made no selection. The project went back for more design work, community input, and analysis.,"},{"id":"passage-72","text":"John J. Kennedy died on July 21, 2022, while serving on the City Council and only weeks after winning reelection. He had represented District 3 since 2013. Justin Jones was appointed later that year to represent District 3 and would take Kennedy’s place in the bridge discussions that followed. He later won the seat in the March 2024 special election."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 5 · 2022. The first design cycle resets","aliases":[],"text":"2022. The first design cycle resets In September 2022, Public Works returned to the Public Safety Committee with a project reset. The first design cycle, which began with the Donald MacDonald Architects contract in May 2019, had produced three vertical-barrier concepts and the full-scale mockups installed in March 2021., The committee had declined to recommend one in August 2021. Afterward, City staff and Pasadena Heritage sought new design input through the Southern California chapter of the Institute of Classical Architecture & Art. By September 2022, staff was preparing to remove the old mockups, restore the temporary fencing where they had stood, and hire a new design consultant. The report’s schedule anticipated a new consultant by December 2022 or January 2023, design development through June, and a return to the committee around September 2023. The next contract didn’t arrive in that window. The first request for proposals drew none, so the City reissued it. Pasadena awarded the new design contract to Apexx Architecture on April 14, 2023, several months after the schedule in the September report., Pasadena had ended one design cycle and was starting another.","route":"paper/2022/","path":"paper/2022/","passages":[{"id":"passage-73","text":"2022. The first design cycle resets"},{"id":"passage-74","text":"In September 2022, Public Works returned to the Public Safety Committee with a project reset. The first design cycle, which began with the Donald MacDonald Architects contract in May 2019, had produced three vertical-barrier concepts and the full-scale mockups installed in March 2021., The committee had declined to recommend one in August 2021."},{"id":"passage-75","text":"Afterward, City staff and Pasadena Heritage sought new design input through the Southern California chapter of the Institute of Classical Architecture & Art. By September 2022, staff was preparing to remove the old mockups, restore the temporary fencing where they had stood, and hire a new design consultant. The report’s schedule anticipated a new consultant by December 2022 or January 2023, design development through June, and a return to the committee around September 2023."},{"id":"passage-76","text":"The next contract didn’t arrive in that window. The first request for proposals drew none, so the City reissued it. Pasadena awarded the new design contract to Apexx Architecture on April 14, 2023, several months after the schedule in the September report., Pasadena had ended one design cycle and was starting another."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 6 · 2023. Previously considered alternatives return","aliases":[],"text":"2023. Previously considered alternatives return Apexx worked with community and preservation representatives and gathered public input as it developed a new set of barrier concepts. By the November Public Safety Committee meeting, three were under consideration. They were canted webmesh, vertical webmesh, and metal pickets. The webmesh designs used metal mesh stretched between posts, either upright or angled. The picket design used a row of closely spaced vertical metal bars., Canted webmesh Vertical webmesh Metal pickets Figure 7. Three proposed barrier concepts presented in November 2023. Renderings of proposed designs, not built conditions. Apexx Architecture / City of Pasadena. At that meeting, the committee returned to questions Pasadena had considered before. Madison, who had moved approval of the task force’s recommendation in 2018, described many suicide attempts as crisis-specific and impulsive and recalled a documentary about people who had survived attempts at the Golden Gate Bridge., Madison turned to the new designs. He said they would “completely change the aesthetic of the bridge,” while also saying the City had to “elevate human safety over aesthetics and objects.” He added, “But if there’s a way to accomplish both goals, I think we’re going to be more successful.” Madison then asked about horizontal netting and landscaping below the bridge. Staff said both had already been studied. Staff explained that the Golden Gate approach did not translate neatly because of the Colorado Street Bridge’s geometry, the remaining fall distance, structural demands, and the appearance of a net below the arches. The 2018 task-force recommendation report said “no verifying scientific data was found” for planting trees below the bridge to “cushion” a landing or discourage an attempt. The same report said trees could not be planted along approximately one-fifth of the span, including its highest point. The task force treated trees as a possible supplement to a vertical barrier., After hearing those answers, Madison wasn’t ready to support any of the three permanent designs. He suggested the temporary fencing might be preferable to what was being proposed and said it would take a significant change in community sentiment for him to support one of the three concepts. Jones was newer to the bridge debate. He hadn’t been part of the Council’s 2018 decision or the committee’s 2021 discussions. At the November 2023 meeting, he relayed concerns from residents who disliked both the temporary fencing and the proposed permanent designs, then asked what other options were available. Staff told him that the alternatives had already been studied extensively and that the Council had previously chosen vertical barriers as the direction., Jones also asked how involved Public Health had been in the new working group and whether restricting access at one site simply shifted attempts elsewhere. Staff pointed to the mental-health specialist in the process, explained that interrupting access to a selected site generally didn’t send people to the next bridge, and agreed to bring health professionals when the project returned. The Council had considered the same substitution question on April 23, 2018, when Public Health said research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed., Gordo returned to the staffing idea he had raised in 2021. Deputy Director of Public Works and City Engineer Kris Markarian said the City had already examined staffing and did not consider it cost-effective. Meaningful coverage would require multiple people and a continuous presence because the bridge is long, curved, and cannot be observed from one point. She also cited liability concerns and incidents outside ordinary working hours. Staff considered stationed personnel a possible supplement to a physical barrier. Gordo asked for a more specific assessment of trained security at night and Park Safety specialists, docents, or volunteers during the day. Madison questioned whether personnel could respond quickly enough because some incidents can “unfold in five minutes.” Gordo asked staff to consult mental-health professionals about whether a visible human presence could deter an impulsive attempt. Near the end of the November 2023 meeting, Public Works Director Tony Olmos and Markarian again explained that a barrier buys time. No permanent design was selected. The committee kept the planned commission reviews and public survey moving while asking staff to return with additional work on staffing and other alternatives., At its December 5, 2023 review, the Historic Preservation Commission recommended vertical webmesh as its first choice and metal pickets as its second.","route":"paper/2023/","path":"paper/2023/","passages":[{"id":"passage-77","text":"2023. Previously considered alternatives return"},{"id":"passage-78","text":"Apexx worked with community and preservation representatives and gathered public input as it developed a new set of barrier concepts. By the November Public Safety Committee meeting, three were under consideration. They were canted webmesh, vertical webmesh, and metal pickets. The webmesh designs used metal mesh stretched between posts, either upright or angled. The picket design used a row of closely spaced vertical metal bars.,"},{"id":"passage-79","text":"Canted webmesh Vertical webmesh Metal pickets"},{"id":"passage-81","text":"Figure 7. Three proposed barrier concepts presented in November 2023. Renderings of proposed designs, not built conditions. Apexx Architecture / City of Pasadena."},{"id":"passage-82","text":"At that meeting, the committee returned to questions Pasadena had considered before. Madison, who had moved approval of the task force’s recommendation in 2018, described many suicide attempts as crisis-specific and impulsive and recalled a documentary about people who had survived attempts at the Golden Gate Bridge.,"},{"id":"passage-83","text":"Madison turned to the new designs. He said they would “completely change the aesthetic of the bridge,” while also saying the City had to “elevate human safety over aesthetics and objects.” He added, “But if there’s a way to accomplish both goals, I think we’re going to be more successful.”"},{"id":"passage-84","text":"Madison then asked about horizontal netting and landscaping below the bridge. Staff said both had already been studied. Staff explained that the Golden Gate approach did not translate neatly because of the Colorado Street Bridge’s geometry, the remaining fall distance, structural demands, and the appearance of a net below the arches. The 2018 task-force recommendation report said “no verifying scientific data was found” for planting trees below the bridge to “cushion” a landing or discourage an attempt. The same report said trees could not be planted along approximately one-fifth of the span, including its highest point. The task force treated trees as a possible supplement to a vertical barrier.,"},{"id":"passage-85","text":"After hearing those answers, Madison wasn’t ready to support any of the three permanent designs. He suggested the temporary fencing might be preferable to what was being proposed and said it would take a significant change in community sentiment for him to support one of the three concepts."},{"id":"passage-86","text":"Jones was newer to the bridge debate. He hadn’t been part of the Council’s 2018 decision or the committee’s 2021 discussions. At the November 2023 meeting, he relayed concerns from residents who disliked both the temporary fencing and the proposed permanent designs, then asked what other options were available. Staff told him that the alternatives had already been studied extensively and that the Council had previously chosen vertical barriers as the direction.,"},{"id":"passage-87","text":"Jones also asked how involved Public Health had been in the new working group and whether restricting access at one site simply shifted attempts elsewhere. Staff pointed to the mental-health specialist in the process, explained that interrupting access to a selected site generally didn’t send people to the next bridge, and agreed to bring health professionals when the project returned. The Council had considered the same substitution question on April 23, 2018, when Public Health said research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed.,"},{"id":"passage-88","text":"Gordo returned to the staffing idea he had raised in 2021. Deputy Director of Public Works and City Engineer Kris Markarian said the City had already examined staffing and did not consider it cost-effective. Meaningful coverage would require multiple people and a continuous presence because the bridge is long, curved, and cannot be observed from one point. She also cited liability concerns and incidents outside ordinary working hours. Staff considered stationed personnel a possible supplement to a physical barrier."},{"id":"passage-89","text":"Gordo asked for a more specific assessment of trained security at night and Park Safety specialists, docents, or volunteers during the day. Madison questioned whether personnel could respond quickly enough because some incidents can “unfold in five minutes.” Gordo asked staff to consult mental-health professionals about whether a visible human presence could deter an impulsive attempt."},{"id":"passage-90","text":"Near the end of the November 2023 meeting, Public Works Director Tony Olmos and Markarian again explained that a barrier buys time."},{"id":"passage-91","text":"No permanent design was selected. The committee kept the planned commission reviews and public survey moving while asking staff to return with additional work on staffing and other alternatives.,"},{"id":"passage-92","text":"At its December 5, 2023 review, the Historic Preservation Commission recommended vertical webmesh as its first choice and metal pickets as its second."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 7 · 2024. The requested answers come back","aliases":[],"text":"2024. The requested answers come back January’s Design Commission review When the designs reached the Design Commission in January 2024, its formal assignment was to advise the Council on aesthetic design concepts. The discussion nevertheless moved beyond the appearance of the three designs and returned to whether other safety measures might work. Chair Julianna Delgado cited the Golden Gate Bridge and asked whether horizontal netting, landscaping, or another measure could be used instead. An off-camera speaker said horizontal netting had been studied since 2017 and traced the City’s vertical-barrier direction to the task-force review and the Council’s 2018 decision. According to the speaker, a net would create construction, rescue, training, equipment, cost, and architectural problems specific to the Colorado Street Bridge. They said the research the City had reviewed supported a vertical barrier as the most effective option. They also noted that the City’s previous consultant, Donald MacDonald Architects, had served as lead designer of the Golden Gate system and had considered dozens of alternatives for Pasadena before the City retained a new consultant.,, Delgado then asked whether the public had been shown a horizontal option. The speaker said it had, but that the Council had directed the City to pursue a vertical barrier. Delgado replied that this was “a previous City Council and a long time ago.” One commissioner said the Golden Gate and Colorado Street bridges were structurally different and that a net below the Colorado Street Bridge would compete with its arches. Delgado argued that a net could be placed farther below, suggested planting cacti beneath the bridge, and repeated a public suggestion to construct apartment buildings there. A responding speaker noted that bungalows already existed below the bridge and said the safer approach was to keep people from leaving the bridge deck. Later in the meeting, Delgado said she couldn’t support any of the three designs. She said there had to be a better solution and that she didn’t want any of the presented barriers on the bridge. Other commissioners expressed different preferences. Pasadena Now reported that Srinivas Rao favored the canted mesh but wanted further refinement. Marie-Claude Fares preferred webmesh and criticized the effect of pickets on views from the bridge and its surroundings. Robert Carpenter said he found it difficult to accept any of the proposals. The commission did not reach consensus on any of the designs. Its advisory comments called for further study of the metal pickets and canted webmesh, including the angle and details of the canted design, and consideration of combining pickets with webmesh. July’s committee review When the project returned to the Public Safety Committee in July 2024, the discussion supplied a more detailed answer to Delgado’s question about horizontal netting. By then, staff’s options had narrowed to vertical webmesh and metal pickets, with canted webmesh listed as eliminated in the July presentation. Staff also returned with much of what elected officials had asked for, including patrol costs, technology analysis, horizontal-netting research, Golden Gate consultation, rescue concerns, survey results, and suicide-prevention expertise. The committee also heard from Bonnie Tang, whose son Nicholas died by suicide at the bridge in April 2021. She shared her family’s loss as part of the presentation. Committee members expressed condolences and thanked her for speaking. Horizontal netting came back with a much fuller engineering and rescue review. The original 2018 task-force comparison had rated it relatively highly for effectiveness but poorly for aesthetics and its impact on emergency services. By 2024, after preliminary site-specific engineering work and direct consultation with Golden Gate Bridge officials, Pasadena’s review concluded that horizontal netting wasn’t a viable solution for this bridge., Fire Chief Chad Augustin explained what a rescue could require. Someone who landed on a net could be 10 or 20 feet below the bridge, making it difficult for police to determine whether the person had a weapon or whether rescuers could safely approach. A rescue could require both of Pasadena’s ladder trucks for hours, along with specialized equipment and additional training. Augustin supported having a barrier on the bridge, but he couldn’t recommend horizontal netting from a public-safety standpoint. 2017 · Safety-netting concept 2024 · Preliminary horizontal-netting Option 1 Figure 8. Horizontal-netting concepts presented in 2017 (top) and 2024 (bottom). Renderings of proposed designs, not built conditions. City of Pasadena. The committee also received the requested patrol costs. The estimate was roughly $600,000 a year for private security and about $1.16 million a year for law-enforcement coverage. Even at those levels, patrol wasn’t considered an effective substitute for a physical barrier. The public survey brought a different kind of answer. It drew 678 responses overall. The City reported that 57% of those answering the need question said a vertical barrier wasn’t needed and 43% said it was. Asked separately whether a vertical barrier would help prevent suicides, 40% said yes, 33% said no, and 27% didn’t know. Some respondents skipped the design ranking, so the overall response count should not be assumed to apply to every question. People chose whether to take the survey, so its results cannot be treated as the views of Pasadena residents as a whole. Staff said 90% of respondents were Pasadena residents, and 74% said they hadn’t participated in the project’s community meetings. Among respondents who ranked the designs, metal pickets came first, while the Historic Preservation Commission had preferred vertical webmesh. Lighting drew broader support. The City reported 81% support for added lighting in the survey, and the commission unanimously supported lighting under the balustrade. Staff saw those results partly as an education problem. Markarian, then acting director of Public Works, said they pointed to a need for “additional education.” Sandri Kramer of Didi Hirsch was even more direct. With roughly 60% of respondents either unconvinced that a vertical barrier would prevent suicides or unsure whether it would, she called it a “public education issue” and explained why restricting access can save lives., Kramer’s explanation centered on the time between suicidal intent and an attempt. Restricting access, she explained, can allow the immediate danger to ease or create an opening for intervention. A separate study of England’s Clifton Suspension Bridge illustrates how that can work. After barriers were installed, bridge staff reported that they had more time to reach people, while deaths fell even though the number of incidents did not. The bridge also used cameras and staff patrols. Members weigh the next step Jones’s questions moved the discussion toward a decision. He asked whether 24/7 patrol was being ruled out because of cost, effectiveness, or both. Staff said both. He then asked whether anyone involved in the expert review had recommended horizontal netting. Staff said no. The recommendation against it had been unanimous. After hearing those answers, Jones said he was prepared to move forward with one of the two remaining designs. Hampton pressed for a faster timetable. He asked staff to return in about 90 days and said vertical mesh was his preferred permanent design. He also raised cameras and motion detection as possible supplements. Later in the same discussion, however, after staff said horizontal netting hadn’t been recommended and Fire had explained its rescue concerns, Hampton said he didn’t want the option taken off the table. He still considered it the most attractive of the alternatives., During that follow-up, Hampton asked about rescue cushions. Fire Chief Chad Augustin said Pasadena’s cushion was rated for roughly 100 feet, while parts of the bridge are about 150 feet high and the equipment couldn’t be positioned everywhere below it. Hampton then asked whether a higher-capacity cushion existed, a question the City hadn’t already answered. Fire did not know and agreed to investigate. A May 2026 Fire Department presentation listed a rescue cushion among its response resources, but gave no capacity rating. That record does not resolve Hampton’s question. Eight months after the November discussion of netting and landscaping, Madison again turned to measures below the bridge. He said the City needed an engineering solution and asked about ground cover, physical impediments, and layered netting. Staff and consultants again explained the limits involving fall distance, structure, cost, and what those measures would do to the appearance of the bridge. Madison also repeated his view that human life and safety had to come before objects., During that exchange, Madison acknowledged that leadership sometimes meant proceeding without a great option. He asked Markarian, “Do you feel like we’ve really done our level best to exhaust all the feasible options?” Markarian described more than seven years on the project and experts with different perspectives. She said, “we have truly exhausted various mitigation measures,” before discussing the Golden Gate comparison. Gordo kept technology in the discussion. He asked about cameras and real-time remote intervention, including ways someone might communicate with a person on the bridge during a crisis. Staff continued to treat those tools as possible supplements to a physical barrier, not replacements for one. By the end of the meeting, the committee had moved in two directions at once. Jones supported pursuing one of the two remaining vertical designs and developing a plan to bring back to the committee. Hampton had named vertical mesh as his preference. But horizontal netting, below-bridge alternatives, and technology were still in the discussion. No permanent design was sent to the full Council. Staff’s proposed schedule already called for further design work, public feedback, and commission review before a return to the committee in early 2025.,,","route":"paper/2024/","path":"paper/2024/","passages":[{"id":"passage-93","text":"2024. The requested answers come back"},{"id":"passage-2024-january-heading","text":"January’s Design Commission review"},{"id":"passage-94","text":"When the designs reached the Design Commission in January 2024, its formal assignment was to advise the Council on aesthetic design concepts. The discussion nevertheless moved beyond the appearance of the three designs and returned to whether other safety measures might work."},{"id":"passage-95","text":"Chair Julianna Delgado cited the Golden Gate Bridge and asked whether horizontal netting, landscaping, or another measure could be used instead. An off-camera speaker said horizontal netting had been studied since 2017 and traced the City’s vertical-barrier direction to the task-force review and the Council’s 2018 decision. According to the speaker, a net would create construction, rescue, training, equipment, cost, and architectural problems specific to the Colorado Street Bridge. They said the research the City had reviewed supported a vertical barrier as the most effective option. They also noted that the City’s previous consultant, Donald MacDonald Architects, had served as lead designer of the Golden Gate system and had considered dozens of alternatives for Pasadena before the City retained a new consultant.,,"},{"id":"passage-96","text":"Delgado then asked whether the public had been shown a horizontal option. The speaker said it had, but that the Council had directed the City to pursue a vertical barrier. Delgado replied that this was “a previous City Council and a long time ago.” One commissioner said the Golden Gate and Colorado Street bridges were structurally different and that a net below the Colorado Street Bridge would compete with its arches. Delgado argued that a net could be placed farther below, suggested planting cacti beneath the bridge, and repeated a public suggestion to construct apartment buildings there. A responding speaker noted that bungalows already existed below the bridge and said the safer approach was to keep people from leaving the bridge deck."},{"id":"passage-97","text":"Later in the meeting, Delgado said she couldn’t support any of the three designs. She said there had to be a better solution and that she didn’t want any of the presented barriers on the bridge."},{"id":"passage-2024-commission-preferences","text":"Other commissioners expressed different preferences. Pasadena Now reported that Srinivas Rao favored the canted mesh but wanted further refinement. Marie-Claude Fares preferred webmesh and criticized the effect of pickets on views from the bridge and its surroundings. Robert Carpenter said he found it difficult to accept any of the proposals."},{"id":"passage-98","text":"The commission did not reach consensus on any of the designs. Its advisory comments called for further study of the metal pickets and canted webmesh, including the angle and details of the canted design, and consideration of combining pickets with webmesh."},{"id":"passage-2024-july-heading","text":"July’s committee review"},{"id":"passage-99","text":"When the project returned to the Public Safety Committee in July 2024, the discussion supplied a more detailed answer to Delgado’s question about horizontal netting. By then, staff’s options had narrowed to vertical webmesh and metal pickets, with canted webmesh listed as eliminated in the July presentation. Staff also returned with much of what elected officials had asked for, including patrol costs, technology analysis, horizontal-netting research, Golden Gate consultation, rescue concerns, survey results, and suicide-prevention expertise."},{"id":"passage-2024-tang","text":"The committee also heard from Bonnie Tang, whose son Nicholas died by suicide at the bridge in April 2021. She shared her family’s loss as part of the presentation. Committee members expressed condolences and thanked her for speaking."},{"id":"passage-100","text":"Horizontal netting came back with a much fuller engineering and rescue review. The original 2018 task-force comparison had rated it relatively highly for effectiveness but poorly for aesthetics and its impact on emergency services. By 2024, after preliminary site-specific engineering work and direct consultation with Golden Gate Bridge officials, Pasadena’s review concluded that horizontal netting wasn’t a viable solution for this bridge.,"},{"id":"passage-101","text":"Fire Chief Chad Augustin explained what a rescue could require. Someone who landed on a net could be 10 or 20 feet below the bridge, making it difficult for police to determine whether the person had a weapon or whether rescuers could safely approach. A rescue could require both of Pasadena’s ladder trucks for hours, along with specialized equipment and additional training. Augustin supported having a barrier on the bridge, but he couldn’t recommend horizontal netting from a public-safety standpoint."},{"id":"passage-102","text":"2017 · Safety-netting concept"},{"id":"passage-104","text":"2024 · Preliminary horizontal-netting Option 1"},{"id":"passage-106","text":"Figure 8. Horizontal-netting concepts presented in 2017 (top) and 2024 (bottom). Renderings of proposed designs, not built conditions. City of Pasadena."},{"id":"passage-107","text":"The committee also received the requested patrol costs. The estimate was roughly $600,000 a year for private security and about $1.16 million a year for law-enforcement coverage. Even at those levels, patrol wasn’t considered an effective substitute for a physical barrier."},{"id":"passage-108","text":"The public survey brought a different kind of answer. It drew 678 responses overall. The City reported that 57% of those answering the need question said a vertical barrier wasn’t needed and 43% said it was. Asked separately whether a vertical barrier would help prevent suicides, 40% said yes, 33% said no, and 27% didn’t know. Some respondents skipped the design ranking, so the overall response count should not be assumed to apply to every question. People chose whether to take the survey, so its results cannot be treated as the views of Pasadena residents as a whole. Staff said 90% of respondents were Pasadena residents, and 74% said they hadn’t participated in the project’s community meetings."},{"id":"passage-109","text":"Among respondents who ranked the designs, metal pickets came first, while the Historic Preservation Commission had preferred vertical webmesh. Lighting drew broader support. The City reported 81% support for added lighting in the survey, and the commission unanimously supported lighting under the balustrade."},{"id":"passage-110","text":"Staff saw those results partly as an education problem. Markarian, then acting director of Public Works, said they pointed to a need for “additional education.” Sandri Kramer of Didi Hirsch was even more direct. With roughly 60% of respondents either unconvinced that a vertical barrier would prevent suicides or unsure whether it would, she called it a “public education issue” and explained why restricting access can save lives.,"},{"id":"passage-111","text":"Kramer’s explanation centered on the time between suicidal intent and an attempt. Restricting access, she explained, can allow the immediate danger to ease or create an opening for intervention."},{"id":"passage-112","text":"A separate study of England’s Clifton Suspension Bridge illustrates how that can work. After barriers were installed, bridge staff reported that they had more time to reach people, while deaths fell even though the number of incidents did not. The bridge also used cameras and staff patrols."},{"id":"passage-2024-next-step-heading","text":"Members weigh the next step"},{"id":"passage-113","text":"Jones’s questions moved the discussion toward a decision. He asked whether 24/7 patrol was being ruled out because of cost, effectiveness, or both. Staff said both. He then asked whether anyone involved in the expert review had recommended horizontal netting. Staff said no. The recommendation against it had been unanimous. After hearing those answers, Jones said he was prepared to move forward with one of the two remaining designs."},{"id":"passage-114","text":"Hampton pressed for a faster timetable. He asked staff to return in about 90 days and said vertical mesh was his preferred permanent design. He also raised cameras and motion detection as possible supplements. Later in the same discussion, however, after staff said horizontal netting hadn’t been recommended and Fire had explained its rescue concerns, Hampton said he didn’t want the option taken off the table. He still considered it the most attractive of the alternatives.,"},{"id":"passage-115","text":"During that follow-up, Hampton asked about rescue cushions. Fire Chief Chad Augustin said Pasadena’s cushion was rated for roughly 100 feet, while parts of the bridge are about 150 feet high and the equipment couldn’t be positioned everywhere below it. Hampton then asked whether a higher-capacity cushion existed, a question the City hadn’t already answered. Fire did not know and agreed to investigate. A May 2026 Fire Department presentation listed a rescue cushion among its response resources, but gave no capacity rating. That record does not resolve Hampton’s question."},{"id":"passage-116","text":"Eight months after the November discussion of netting and landscaping, Madison again turned to measures below the bridge. He said the City needed an engineering solution and asked about ground cover, physical impediments, and layered netting. Staff and consultants again explained the limits involving fall distance, structure, cost, and what those measures would do to the appearance of the bridge. Madison also repeated his view that human life and safety had to come before objects.,"},{"id":"passage-117","text":"During that exchange, Madison acknowledged that leadership sometimes meant proceeding without a great option. He asked Markarian, “Do you feel like we’ve really done our level best to exhaust all the feasible options?” Markarian described more than seven years on the project and experts with different perspectives. She said, “we have truly exhausted various mitigation measures,” before discussing the Golden Gate comparison."},{"id":"passage-118","text":"Gordo kept technology in the discussion. He asked about cameras and real-time remote intervention, including ways someone might communicate with a person on the bridge during a crisis. Staff continued to treat those tools as possible supplements to a physical barrier, not replacements for one."},{"id":"passage-119","text":"By the end of the meeting, the committee had moved in two directions at once. Jones supported pursuing one of the two remaining vertical designs and developing a plan to bring back to the committee. Hampton had named vertical mesh as his preference. But horizontal netting, below-bridge alternatives, and technology were still in the discussion. No permanent design was sent to the full Council. Staff’s proposed schedule already called for further design work, public feedback, and commission review before a return to the committee in early 2025.,,"}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 8 · Questions that returned","aliases":[],"text":"The record by July 2024 Questions that returned Some discussions revisited earlier findings. Others led to more detailed cost, engineering, and rescue analysis. Selected discussions. Dates identify the records summarized, not necessarily the first time a question was raised. Would deaths simply move elsewhere? April 2018 Council recording. Public Health said research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed, rather than a simple shift elsewhere. November 2023 committee recording. The substitution question returned. Staff answered and agreed to bring health professionals to the next review. Presentation and recording. A suicide-prevention specialist explained how restricting access creates time for a crisis to ease or for intervention. This was a fuller explanation of the mechanism, not a new study of displacement. Why not horizontal netting? April 2018 evaluation matrix. Netting rated relatively highly for effectiveness but poorly for appearance and emergency-services impact. November 2023, January 2024, and July 2024 recordings. Netting returned as an alternative to the proposed vertical designs. Presentation and recording. Bridge-specific analysis, Golden Gate consultation, and Fire’s review supported a recommendation against netting. Staff reported unanimity among reviewers. Engineering remained preliminary. Could trees or landscaping replace a barrier? April 2018 report. No verifying scientific data was found for planting to cushion a landing or discourage an attempt. Trees were treated as a possible supplement, not a replacement. November 2023, January 2024, and July 2024 recordings. Suggestions included trees, landscaping, ground cover, and other measures below the bridge. Recording. Further below-bridge proposals prompted discussion of fall distance, structure, cost, and appearance. These were varied proposals, not one identical question repeated each time. Could staffing or patrols replace a barrier? 2018 task-force materials. Staffing was considered a complementary measure alongside physical access restriction. August 2021 and November 2023 recordings. Members explored staffing both as an alternative and as a supplement. Later requests sought more detail about coverage, cost, and deterrence. Presentation and recording. Annual estimates were about $600,000 for private security and $1.16 million for law enforcement. Staff ruled out patrol as a replacement on cost and effectiveness grounds. What about cameras and remote intervention? 2018 task-force materials. Technology was considered alongside a physical barrier, not as its replacement. July 2024 recording. Members raised cameras, motion detection, and communication with someone on the bridge during a crisis. Recording. These remained possible supplements to a physical barrier. The discussion did not establish them as replacements.","route":"paper/questions/","path":"paper/questions/","passages":[{"id":"passage-questions-1","text":"The record by July 2024"},{"id":"passage-questions-2","text":"Questions that returned"},{"id":"passage-questions-3","text":"Some discussions revisited earlier findings. Others led to more detailed cost, engineering, and rescue analysis."},{"id":"csb-questions-note","text":"Selected discussions. Dates identify the records summarized, not necessarily the first time a question was raised."},{"id":"passage-questions-4","text":"Would deaths simply move elsewhere?"},{"id":"passage-questions-5","text":"April 2018 Council recording. Public Health said research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed, rather than a simple shift elsewhere."},{"id":"passage-questions-6","text":"November 2023 committee recording. The substitution question returned. Staff answered and agreed to bring health professionals to the next review."},{"id":"passage-questions-7","text":"Presentation and recording. A suicide-prevention specialist explained how restricting access creates time for a crisis to ease or for intervention. This was a fuller explanation of the mechanism, not a new study of displacement."},{"id":"passage-questions-8","text":"Why not horizontal netting?"},{"id":"passage-questions-9","text":"April 2018 evaluation matrix. Netting rated relatively highly for effectiveness but poorly for appearance and emergency-services impact."},{"id":"passage-questions-10","text":"November 2023, January 2024, and July 2024 recordings. Netting returned as an alternative to the proposed vertical designs."},{"id":"passage-questions-11","text":"Presentation and recording. Bridge-specific analysis, Golden Gate consultation, and Fire’s review supported a recommendation against netting. Staff reported unanimity among reviewers. Engineering remained preliminary."},{"id":"passage-questions-12","text":"Could trees or landscaping replace a barrier?"},{"id":"passage-questions-13","text":"April 2018 report. No verifying scientific data was found for planting to cushion a landing or discourage an attempt. Trees were treated as a possible supplement, not a replacement."},{"id":"passage-questions-14","text":"November 2023, January 2024, and July 2024 recordings. Suggestions included trees, landscaping, ground cover, and other measures below the bridge."},{"id":"passage-questions-15","text":"Recording. Further below-bridge proposals prompted discussion of fall distance, structure, cost, and appearance. These were varied proposals, not one identical question repeated each time."},{"id":"passage-questions-16","text":"Could staffing or patrols replace a barrier?"},{"id":"passage-questions-17","text":"2018 task-force materials. Staffing was considered a complementary measure alongside physical access restriction."},{"id":"passage-questions-18","text":"August 2021 and November 2023 recordings. Members explored staffing both as an alternative and as a supplement. Later requests sought more detail about coverage, cost, and deterrence."},{"id":"passage-questions-19","text":"Presentation and recording. Annual estimates were about $600,000 for private security and $1.16 million for law enforcement. Staff ruled out patrol as a replacement on cost and effectiveness grounds."},{"id":"passage-questions-20","text":"What about cameras and remote intervention?"},{"id":"passage-questions-21","text":"2018 task-force materials. Technology was considered alongside a physical barrier, not as its replacement."},{"id":"passage-questions-22","text":"July 2024 recording. Members raised cameras, motion detection, and communication with someone on the bridge during a crisis."},{"id":"passage-questions-23","text":"Recording. These remained possible supplements to a physical barrier. The discussion did not establish them as replacements."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 9 · 2025–2026. The schedule moves again","aliases":[],"text":"2025–2026. The schedule moves again The July 2024 timeline didn’t hold. Staff had laid out a probable schedule for refining the concepts through the summer and fall, returning to the Historic Preservation and Design commissions, and coming back to the committee early in 2025. By June 2025, construction of new test panels had only begun. In July, the San Rafael Neighborhoods Association publicly pressed the City to make a decision. Responding to Pasadena Now, City Engineer Brent Maue said full-scale mockups were being built for internal evaluation. He anticipated presentations to the Design Commission, Public Safety Committee, and City Council beginning later that summer and continuing into fall. Installation of mockups on the bridge for public feedback would depend on those reviews. Pasadena’s fiscal year 2026 ran from July 2025 through June 2026. In its first-quarter report for fiscal year 2026, the City anticipated a final recommendation to the Public Safety Committee in the second quarter and moved the design phase’s target completion date from June 30, 2026, to June 30, 2028. A February 2026 second-quarter report said the test panels and review were complete, but presentations to the West Pasadena Residents’ Association and the Public Safety Committee were then anticipated in the third quarter. The June 30, 2028 target remained. The project’s appropriations reached $3,050,000, with $950,000 from the General Fund, $100,000 in Gas Tax funding, and $2 million labeled ARPA. In 2025, the Council transferred $130,000 on June 9 and $46,000 on July 21 to the San Rafael Bridge Seismic Retrofit project, reducing the total to $2,874,000. Staff said enough remained to finish design without affecting its progress. Construction still required additional funding. At a special joint Finance Committee and City Council meeting on April 20, 2026, staff reported about $1.5 million spent against the design-phase project account, about $1.3 million left to finish it, and a separate construction estimate of roughly $4 million. The records reviewed here do not show construction funding confirmed. That account paid for more than architects’ fees. The August 2021 report describes earlier spending on temporary fencing and maintenance, mockups, environmental assessment, conceptual design, and outreach. The report through June 30, 2026 records $1,475,508 in life-to-date spending against project 73324. It does not divide that total among those uses. Early in the meeting, Acting City Manager Matt Hawkesworth raised the possibility of redirecting the unspent bridge money to other priorities. He said Pasadena had made “the shortlist for an earmark potentially in the next budget cycle” for $4.5 million. Public Works Director Greg de Vinck described it as a shortlisted grant application and said redirecting the bridge money would leave Pasadena without the required matching funds. Later, Councilmember Rick Cole asked how much Pasadena had already spent and whether the remaining money would pay for a finished barrier. City Engineer Brent Maue said the balance was expected to finish the design, not build the barrier. He estimated construction at roughly $4 million. Federal funding remained uncertain. Rep. Judy Chu’s published request for fiscal year 2027 listed $4 million for the bridge. A House committee report dated June 5, 2026, recommended $1 million. The records do not establish whether the $4.5 million discussed in April referred to that request or what “shortlist” meant. Pasadena’s project page continued to say construction funding hadn’t been identified., The schedule slipped again. In its third-quarter report for fiscal year 2026, the City pushed the expected Public Safety Committee presentation into the fourth quarter. The year-end report, covering work through June 30, said Pasadena Heritage was still reviewing the concepts and listed the design phase as ongoing. Pasadena Heritage helped develop and review concepts, but a permanent design still needed City Council approval. By then, the City had spent $1,475,508, or 51% of the $2,874,000 set aside for the design-phase project. Its target completion date remained June 30, 2028, but the report no longer said when the project would return to the committee. The Public Safety Committee has a new chair, Vice Mayor Jess Rivas, with Hampton, Jones, and Madison remaining as members. The official records reviewed here show no final permanent-design approval or construction award.,,","route":"paper/2025-2026/","path":"paper/2025-2026/","passages":[{"id":"passage-120","text":"2025–2026. The schedule moves again"},{"id":"passage-121","text":"The July 2024 timeline didn’t hold. Staff had laid out a probable schedule for refining the concepts through the summer and fall, returning to the Historic Preservation and Design commissions, and coming back to the committee early in 2025. By June 2025, construction of new test panels had only begun."},{"id":"passage-2025-city-response","text":"In July, the San Rafael Neighborhoods Association publicly pressed the City to make a decision. Responding to Pasadena Now, City Engineer Brent Maue said full-scale mockups were being built for internal evaluation. He anticipated presentations to the Design Commission, Public Safety Committee, and City Council beginning later that summer and continuing into fall. Installation of mockups on the bridge for public feedback would depend on those reviews."},{"id":"passage-2025-quarterly-reports","text":"Pasadena’s fiscal year 2026 ran from July 2025 through June 2026. In its first-quarter report for fiscal year 2026, the City anticipated a final recommendation to the Public Safety Committee in the second quarter and moved the design phase’s target completion date from June 30, 2026, to June 30, 2028. A February 2026 second-quarter report said the test panels and review were complete, but presentations to the West Pasadena Residents’ Association and the Public Safety Committee were then anticipated in the third quarter. The June 30, 2028 target remained."},{"id":"passage-122","text":"The project’s appropriations reached $3,050,000, with $950,000 from the General Fund, $100,000 in Gas Tax funding, and $2 million labeled ARPA. In 2025, the Council transferred $130,000 on June 9 and $46,000 on July 21 to the San Rafael Bridge Seismic Retrofit project, reducing the total to $2,874,000. Staff said enough remained to finish design without affecting its progress. Construction still required additional funding."},{"id":"passage-123","text":"At a special joint Finance Committee and City Council meeting on April 20, 2026, staff reported about $1.5 million spent against the design-phase project account, about $1.3 million left to finish it, and a separate construction estimate of roughly $4 million. The records reviewed here do not show construction funding confirmed."},{"id":"passage-124","text":"That account paid for more than architects’ fees. The August 2021 report describes earlier spending on temporary fencing and maintenance, mockups, environmental assessment, conceptual design, and outreach. The report through June 30, 2026 records $1,475,508 in life-to-date spending against project 73324. It does not divide that total among those uses."},{"id":"passage-125","text":"Early in the meeting, Acting City Manager Matt Hawkesworth raised the possibility of redirecting the unspent bridge money to other priorities. He said Pasadena had made “the shortlist for an earmark potentially in the next budget cycle” for $4.5 million. Public Works Director Greg de Vinck described it as a shortlisted grant application and said redirecting the bridge money would leave Pasadena without the required matching funds."},{"id":"passage-126","text":"Later, Councilmember Rick Cole asked how much Pasadena had already spent and whether the remaining money would pay for a finished barrier. City Engineer Brent Maue said the balance was expected to finish the design, not build the barrier. He estimated construction at roughly $4 million."},{"id":"passage-127","text":"Federal funding remained uncertain. Rep. Judy Chu’s published request for fiscal year 2027 listed $4 million for the bridge. A House committee report dated June 5, 2026, recommended $1 million. The records do not establish whether the $4.5 million discussed in April referred to that request or what “shortlist” meant. Pasadena’s project page continued to say construction funding hadn’t been identified.,"},{"id":"passage-128","text":"The schedule slipped again. In its third-quarter report for fiscal year 2026, the City pushed the expected Public Safety Committee presentation into the fourth quarter. The year-end report, covering work through June 30, said Pasadena Heritage was still reviewing the concepts and listed the design phase as ongoing. Pasadena Heritage helped develop and review concepts, but a permanent design still needed City Council approval. By then, the City had spent $1,475,508, or 51% of the $2,874,000 set aside for the design-phase project. Its target completion date remained June 30, 2028, but the report no longer said when the project would return to the committee."},{"id":"passage-129","text":"The Public Safety Committee has a new chair, Vice Mayor Jess Rivas, with Hampton, Jones, and Madison remaining as members. The official records reviewed here show no final permanent-design approval or construction award.,,"}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 10 · How the forecasts changed","aliases":[],"text":"Published schedules How the forecasts changed The City’s quarterly reports anticipated bringing the project back to the Public Safety Committee in October – December 2025, then January – March 2026, and then April – June 2026. The report covering the fiscal year ending June 30, 2026, gave no committee return date. Construction, design approval, and a committee return are distinct milestones. Later forecasts are not completed events. May 2019 Construction around August 2020, provided the City approved additional construction funding. The August 2021 review ended without a design recommendation. The City project page still identified construction funding as unresolved when checked in September 2026. February 2020 Committee recommendation and Council approval in May 2020. Full-scale mockups were installed in March 2021. At the August 2021 review, the committee did not recommend a permanent design. September 2022 A return to the committee around September 2023. The project returned on November 15, 2023. July 2024 A return to the committee in early 2025, after further design work, public feedback, and commission review. The fiscal 2026 first-quarter report anticipated a committee return in October – December 2025. FY2026 first-quarter report Committee return in the second quarter, October – December 2025. The second-quarter report anticipated a return in January – March 2026. FY2026 second-quarter report Committee return in the third quarter, January – March 2026. The third-quarter report anticipated a return in April – June 2026. FY2026 third-quarter report Committee return in the fourth quarter, April – June 2026. The year-end report gave no committee return date. It continued to list the design phase as ongoing. The June 30, 2028 target concerns completion of design.","route":"paper/forecasts/","path":"paper/forecasts/","passages":[{"id":"passage-forecasts-1","text":"Published schedules"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-2","text":"How the forecasts changed"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-3","text":"The City’s quarterly reports anticipated bringing the project back to the Public Safety Committee in October – December 2025, then January – March 2026, and then April – June 2026. The report covering the fiscal year ending June 30, 2026, gave no committee return date."},{"id":"csb-forecasts-note","text":"Construction, design approval, and a committee return are distinct milestones. Later forecasts are not completed events."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-4","text":"May 2019"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-5","text":"Construction around August 2020, provided the City approved additional construction funding."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-6","text":"The August 2021 review ended without a design recommendation. The City project page still identified construction funding as unresolved when checked in September 2026."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-7","text":"February 2020"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-8","text":"Committee recommendation and Council approval in May 2020."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-9","text":"Full-scale mockups were installed in March 2021. At the August 2021 review, the committee did not recommend a permanent design."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-10","text":"September 2022"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-11","text":"A return to the committee around September 2023."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-12","text":"The project returned on November 15, 2023."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-13","text":"July 2024"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-14","text":"A return to the committee in early 2025, after further design work, public feedback, and commission review."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-15","text":"The fiscal 2026 first-quarter report anticipated a committee return in October – December 2025."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-16","text":"FY2026 first-quarter report"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-17","text":"Committee return in the second quarter, October – December 2025."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-18","text":"The second-quarter report anticipated a return in January – March 2026."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-19","text":"FY2026 second-quarter report"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-20","text":"Committee return in the third quarter, January – March 2026."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-21","text":"The third-quarter report anticipated a return in April – June 2026."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-22","text":"FY2026 third-quarter report"},{"id":"passage-forecasts-23","text":"Committee return in the fourth quarter, April – June 2026."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-24","text":"The year-end report gave no committee return date. It continued to list the design phase as ongoing."},{"id":"passage-forecasts-25","text":"The June 30, 2028 target concerns completion of design."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 11 · Not every delay is the same","aliases":[],"text":"Not every delay is the same Not all of the time this project has taken is attributable to officials revisiting old questions. Work on a protected historic structure had to account for anchoring, maintenance, emergency access, whether the changes could later be undone, and historic character. COVID-19 delayed material procurement and mockup fabrication, and the first request for a replacement design consultant produced no proposals.,, The pandemic also affected planned funding. In April 2020, the City dropped a proposed new $2 million General Fund appropriation for the bridge from its fiscal year 2021 capital budget to preserve resources for essential services. The adopted budget added no new appropriation for the project and said design would continue, with construction to begin when funds became available. How to pay for construction remained unresolved. Pasadena had funded the design work, but building the permanent barrier would require separate funding. The Eaton Fire required a major City emergency response in January 2025, though the bridge records reviewed here do not attribute a specific project delay to it.,, The record also shows real progress. Pasadena chose a physical barrier, funded design work, hired specialists, developed full-scale mockups, and shortened the design schedule by 10 months. Former Pasadena Heritage executive director Sue Mossman later wrote that the process had taken “much longer than anticipated” and that, after so many options and trial runs, “no solution is going to be perfect.”,, The harder part is what happened after those early decisions. Again and again, staff returned with work meant to narrow the choices. Some questions produced new information. Others brought the committee back to alternatives the City had already studied. A difficult project can take time without every additional round of review being equally necessary. By now, Pasadena has answers to many of the questions that once made this project uncertain. The Council chose a physical barrier as the main suicide-prevention measure. Staff assessed patrol costs and concluded that stationed personnel could supplement a physical barrier but could not replace one. Horizontal netting ran into engineering and rescue problems specific to this bridge. The 2024 survey also showed substantial doubt among respondents about whether a vertical barrier was needed or would work.,,,, By the later meetings, the differences were increasingly about what officials did after they got an answer. Jones moved toward a choice. Hampton named vertical mesh as his preferred design but asked that horizontal netting stay on the table. Madison continued to press for measures below the bridge. Gordo kept staffing and technology in the discussion.,,,,, There had been a warning early on about where this could go. In 2018, Mayor Terry Tornek said, “This is probably the highest level of consensus we’ll achieve in this process, I predict.” He said consensus would become much more challenging to achieve once specific designs were rolled out. A few minutes later, as councilmembers debated height and curvature, he called it “a harbinger of the discussion to come.” More than eight years have passed since the Council’s April 2018 decision. That span reflects, in part, the difficulty of turning agreement on protection into agreement on a design.","route":"paper/delays/","path":"paper/delays/","passages":[{"id":"passage-130","text":"Not every delay is the same"},{"id":"passage-131","text":"Not all of the time this project has taken is attributable to officials revisiting old questions. Work on a protected historic structure had to account for anchoring, maintenance, emergency access, whether the changes could later be undone, and historic character. COVID-19 delayed material procurement and mockup fabrication, and the first request for a replacement design consultant produced no proposals.,,"},{"id":"passage-2020-budget","text":"The pandemic also affected planned funding. In April 2020, the City dropped a proposed new $2 million General Fund appropriation for the bridge from its fiscal year 2021 capital budget to preserve resources for essential services. The adopted budget added no new appropriation for the project and said design would continue, with construction to begin when funds became available."},{"id":"passage-132","text":"How to pay for construction remained unresolved. Pasadena had funded the design work, but building the permanent barrier would require separate funding. The Eaton Fire required a major City emergency response in January 2025, though the bridge records reviewed here do not attribute a specific project delay to it.,,"},{"id":"passage-133","text":"The record also shows real progress. Pasadena chose a physical barrier, funded design work, hired specialists, developed full-scale mockups, and shortened the design schedule by 10 months. Former Pasadena Heritage executive director Sue Mossman later wrote that the process had taken “much longer than anticipated” and that, after so many options and trial runs, “no solution is going to be perfect.”,,"},{"id":"passage-134","text":"The harder part is what happened after those early decisions. Again and again, staff returned with work meant to narrow the choices. Some questions produced new information. Others brought the committee back to alternatives the City had already studied. A difficult project can take time without every additional round of review being equally necessary."},{"id":"passage-135","text":"By now, Pasadena has answers to many of the questions that once made this project uncertain. The Council chose a physical barrier as the main suicide-prevention measure. Staff assessed patrol costs and concluded that stationed personnel could supplement a physical barrier but could not replace one. Horizontal netting ran into engineering and rescue problems specific to this bridge. The 2024 survey also showed substantial doubt among respondents about whether a vertical barrier was needed or would work.,,,,"},{"id":"passage-136","text":"By the later meetings, the differences were increasingly about what officials did after they got an answer. Jones moved toward a choice. Hampton named vertical mesh as his preferred design but asked that horizontal netting stay on the table. Madison continued to press for measures below the bridge. Gordo kept staffing and technology in the discussion.,,,,,"},{"id":"passage-137","text":"There had been a warning early on about where this could go. In 2018, Mayor Terry Tornek said, “This is probably the highest level of consensus we’ll achieve in this process, I predict.” He said consensus would become much more challenging to achieve once specific designs were rolled out. A few minutes later, as councilmembers debated height and curvature, he called it “a harbinger of the discussion to come.”"},{"id":"passage-138","text":"More than eight years have passed since the Council’s April 2018 decision. That span reflects, in part, the difficulty of turning agreement on protection into agreement on a design."}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 12 · Where that leaves Pasadena","aliases":[],"text":"Where that leaves Pasadena Fewer people died at the bridge in the first few years after the fence went up. Those lower counts were recorded while access remained physically restricted. They do not establish what would happen without the fence., At the November 2023 committee meeting, a Police Department lieutenant reported, in an oral update, deaths at the bridge in 2022 and 2023, showing that the risk had not disappeared. Auckland, New Zealand, offers a documented warning. Grafton Bridge had suicide barriers for decades before the city removed them in 1996 amid concerns about their appearance, effects on the historic structure, and interference with rescue operations. Researchers recorded three deaths from the bridge in 1992–1995, before removal, and 15 in 1997–2000, after removal. After improved curved-glass barriers were installed in 2003, researchers recorded no suicides from the bridge during the following four years, describing the sequence as a natural experiment in what happens when a physical deterrent is present, removed, and restored. Low death counts may show that a barrier is working. They do not mean a bridge has become permanently “decoupled” from the risk of suicide. A city shouldn’t need another rise in deaths to remember why access was restricted in the first place. The bridge’s appearance remains at the center of Pasadena’s debate., The record increasingly suggests that Pasadena’s unresolved question is how to maintain effective protection and public support for it, whether the City keeps the existing fence or replaces it. Keeping the existing fence in place while Pasadena considers its next step is also a choice. The chain-link fence was installed in response to an emergency. It was not the finished product of the design process that followed., The funding discussion shows how a temporary arrangement could become a lasting one. In 2025, the City transferred $176,000 from the design-phase account to another bridge project, though staff said enough remained to finish design. In April 2026, further reallocation was discussed, and Public Works warned that it would leave Pasadena without matching funds for a shortlisted application. Construction funding remained unresolved. Without a funded path to a permanent barrier, the temporary fence could become the long-term answer by default.,, If Pasadena chooses to retain it, the public needs a clear explanation of that choice. Otherwise, the emergency fence risks becoming the public’s picture of what a suicide barrier has to look like. Frustration with its appearance could then become pressure to remove or weaken the protection it provides., That is where the unresolved design debate becomes a concern about safety. The longer the temporary fence remains, the easier it becomes to see the fence as the problem. Its effect on the bridge is visible every day. The deaths it may prevent are not.,","route":"paper/where-that-leaves-pasadena/","path":"paper/where-that-leaves-pasadena/","passages":[{"id":"passage-139","text":"Where that leaves Pasadena"},{"id":"passage-140","text":"Fewer people died at the bridge in the first few years after the fence went up. Those lower counts were recorded while access remained physically restricted. They do not establish what would happen without the fence., At the November 2023 committee meeting, a Police Department lieutenant reported, in an oral update, deaths at the bridge in 2022 and 2023, showing that the risk had not disappeared."},{"id":"passage-141","text":"Auckland, New Zealand, offers a documented warning. Grafton Bridge had suicide barriers for decades before the city removed them in 1996 amid concerns about their appearance, effects on the historic structure, and interference with rescue operations. Researchers recorded three deaths from the bridge in 1992–1995, before removal, and 15 in 1997–2000, after removal. After improved curved-glass barriers were installed in 2003, researchers recorded no suicides from the bridge during the following four years, describing the sequence as a natural experiment in what happens when a physical deterrent is present, removed, and restored. Low death counts may show that a barrier is working. They do not mean a bridge has become permanently “decoupled” from the risk of suicide."},{"id":"passage-142","text":"A city shouldn’t need another rise in deaths to remember why access was restricted in the first place."},{"id":"passage-143","text":"The bridge’s appearance remains at the center of Pasadena’s debate.,"},{"id":"passage-144","text":"The record increasingly suggests that Pasadena’s unresolved question is how to maintain effective protection and public support for it, whether the City keeps the existing fence or replaces it. Keeping the existing fence in place while Pasadena considers its next step is also a choice. The chain-link fence was installed in response to an emergency. It was not the finished product of the design process that followed.,"},{"id":"passage-145","text":"The funding discussion shows how a temporary arrangement could become a lasting one. In 2025, the City transferred $176,000 from the design-phase account to another bridge project, though staff said enough remained to finish design. In April 2026, further reallocation was discussed, and Public Works warned that it would leave Pasadena without matching funds for a shortlisted application. Construction funding remained unresolved. Without a funded path to a permanent barrier, the temporary fence could become the long-term answer by default.,,"},{"id":"passage-146","text":"If Pasadena chooses to retain it, the public needs a clear explanation of that choice. Otherwise, the emergency fence risks becoming the public’s picture of what a suicide barrier has to look like. Frustration with its appearance could then become pressure to remove or weaken the protection it provides.,"},{"id":"passage-147","text":"That is where the unresolved design debate becomes a concern about safety. The longer the temporary fence remains, the easier it becomes to see the fence as the problem. Its effect on the bridge is visible every day. The deaths it may prevent are not.,"}]},{"type":"Paper","title":"Section 13 · In memory","aliases":[],"text":"In memory This paper is dedicated to the people who died by suicide at the Colorado Street Bridge, including those whose names are not included in the surviving public record. If you or someone you know is struggling or in crisis, call or text 988 to reach the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline.","route":"paper/in-memory/","path":"paper/in-memory/","passages":[{"id":"passage-148","text":"In memory"},{"id":"passage-149","text":"This paper is dedicated to the people who died by suicide at the Colorado Street Bridge, including those whose names are not included in the surviving public record."},{"id":"passage-150","text":"If you or someone you know is struggling or in crisis, call or text 988 to reach the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline."}]}];

const speakerTopics={"all":"All topics","design":"Design and direction","staffing":"Staffing and patrol","landscaping":"Landscaping","netting":"Netting and rescue","technology":"Technology","effectiveness":"Effectiveness and substitution","funding":"Funding and project costs"};
const speakers={"hawkesworth":{"name":"Matt Hawkesworth","role":"Acting city manager","remarks":[{"id":"hawkesworth-bridge-funds","title":"Considering other uses for unspent Bridge money","meeting":"v2026","time":"00:08:10","topic":"funding","quote":null,"context":"Hawkesworth raised the possibility of redirecting unspent Bridge money to other priorities. He also said the City had made a shortlist for a possible $4.5 million federal earmark in the next budget cycle.","earlier":"The Council had funded the design-phase project. Two approved transfers in 2025 had reduced its budget to $2,874,000. Construction required separate funding.","response":"Public Works Director Greg de Vinck responded that redirecting the remaining money would leave the City without the matching funds needed for the shortlisted application. This exchange discussed a possible reallocation, not an adopted decision to abandon the project.","outcome":"The records reviewed do not identify April’s federal program or reconcile its $4.5 million with Rep. Chu’s separate $4 million disclosure.","links":[{"label":"de Vinck’s response","source":"v2026","time":"00:09:15"},{"label":"Available-balance sheet","source":"balance2026","page":2},{"label":"Approved budget and transfers","source":"fundingRecords"}],"refs":"31, 41","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the official recording. The speaker and approximate starting time were checked in the earlier April recording review. The shortlist was not a funding award.","sortDate":"2026-04-20","date":"April 20, 2026","body":"Finance Committee / City Council"}]},"devinck":{"name":"Greg de Vinck","role":"Public Works director","remarks":[{"id":"devinck-matching-funds","title":"Explaining why the remaining money mattered to a funding application","meeting":"v2026","time":"00:09:15","topic":"funding","quote":null,"context":"de Vinck described a shortlisted grant application and said redirecting the Bridge money would leave Pasadena without the required matching funds.","earlier":"Hawkesworth had just raised possible reallocation and described a $4.5 million federal earmark shortlist. The existing project budget covered design-phase work, not a funded construction contract.","response":"The April available-balance sheet associates project 73324 with $1,336,791 and a note about retaining funds for a match and final design. It does not identify the federal program, establish the required matching amount, or explain how the balance was calculated.","outcome":"The funding-program linkage and project-level balance calculation remain unresolved.","links":[{"label":"Hawkesworth’s preceding discussion","source":"v2026","time":"00:08:10"},{"label":"Available-balance sheet","source":"balance2026","page":2},{"label":"Design and construction explanation","source":"v2026","time":"02:00:24"}],"refs":"31","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the official recording, using the earlier checked speaker and approximate starting time. The balance-sheet row was visually verified on September 21, 2026. Its amount is a dated report figure, not a current available balance.","sortDate":"2026-04-20","date":"April 20, 2026","body":"Finance Committee / City Council"}]},"cole":{"name":"Rick Cole","role":"Councilmember","remarks":[{"id":"cole-spending-and-completion","title":"Asking what had been spent and what the remaining money would finish","meeting":"v2026","time":"01:59:13","topic":"funding","quote":null,"context":"Cole asked how much Pasadena had already spent and whether the remaining money would pay for a completed barrier.","earlier":"The meeting’s earlier discussion had addressed possible reallocation and matching funds. The design-phase account also covered earlier fencing, maintenance, mockups, environmental assessment, and outreach, not only architects’ fees.","response":"City Engineer Brent Maue explained that the remaining money was expected to finish design, not construction. Staff described about $1.5 million spent, about $1.3 million remaining for design, and a separate construction estimate of roughly $4 million.","outcome":"This exchange distinguished the design account from the additional money needed to build the barrier.","links":[{"label":"Maue’s explanation","source":"v2026","time":"02:00:24"},{"label":"Construction estimate","source":"v2026","time":"02:00:54"},{"label":"Earlier account uses","source":"r2021","page":5}],"refs":"9, 31","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the official recording. Speaker assignments and the approximate starting times come from the earlier checked April passages. The rounded oral figures are not a project-accounting reconciliation.","sortDate":"2026-04-20","date":"April 20, 2026","body":"Finance Committee / City Council"}]},"maue":{"name":"Brent Maue","role":"City engineer","remarks":[{"id":"maue-design-not-construction","title":"Distinguishing the cost of finishing design from building the barrier","meeting":"v2026","time":"02:00:24","topic":"funding","quote":null,"context":"Maue said the remaining balance was expected to finish design, not build the permanent barrier. He then gave a construction estimate of roughly $4 million at 02:00:54 and discussed the $4.5 million shortlist at 02:01:11.","earlier":"Cole had asked whether the money left in the project account would deliver a finished barrier. Earlier in the meeting, staff had raised the need to retain matching funds.","response":"The design balance, construction estimate, and possible federal funding were different amounts at different stages. The reviewed records do not identify April’s federal program or establish that its $4.5 million was the same request as Rep. Chu’s published $4 million request.","outcome":"A later House committee report recommended $1 million, which was not an award or secured construction funding.","links":[{"label":"Cole’s question","source":"v2026","time":"01:59:13"},{"label":"Construction estimate","source":"v2026","time":"02:00:54"},{"label":"Shortlist discussion","source":"v2026","time":"02:01:11"},{"label":"Available-balance sheet","source":"balance2026","page":2}],"refs":"31, 34","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the official recording, using the earlier checked speaker and approximate starting times. An estimate, a funding request, and an award are separate stages.","sortDate":"2026-04-20","date":"April 20, 2026","body":"Finance Committee / City Council"}]},"madison":{"name":"Steve Madison","remarks":[{"id":"madison-2018-motion","sortOrder":1,"title":"Moving the permanent-barrier recommendation","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:39:51","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Madison moved approval of the task-force recommendation. Hampton seconded it, and the Council approved it unanimously.","earlier":"The multidisciplinary task force had considered physical and complementary measures, including netting, landscaping, staffing, and technology.","response":"The adopted direction centered on a permanent physical barrier. The detailed design still had to be developed.","outcome":"This establishes the earlier policy choice against which later design and alternatives discussions can be read.","links":[{"label":"Adopted motion","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Task-force recommendation","source":"r2018"}],"refs":"5–7","kind":"Recorded action","basis":"The action is documented in the official Council minutes, page 5, and retained Source 6. The time marks the approximate motion-and-approval sequence, not a verbatim statement by Madison.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"madison-coupling","title":"Explaining why interrupting access can matter","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:03:49","topic":"effectiveness","quote":"really disruptive action is required to be able to prevent the suicide","context":"Madison discussed Malcolm Gladwell’s account of coupling between a place and a method and explained why disrupting that relationship mattered to the bridge.","earlier":"Public Health had addressed regional effects and substitution during the April 2018 Council discussion.","response":"Madison treated prevention as an important goal while also saying the permanent design was not ready for a decision.","outcome":"He proposed narrowing the field to two choices and obtaining more community input. Gladwell’s general-reader account is separate from the original intervention studies.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Public Health discussion","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"},{"label":"Madison on narrowing the options","source":"v2021","time":"01:08:35"}],"refs":"11, 32","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-two-options","title":"Narrow to two choices, then seek more input","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:08:35","topic":"design","quote":"what we should try to do is get down to two choices","context":"Madison said the City had either not found the right solution or had not had enough process to identify it. He proposed narrowing the alternatives before returning to the community.","earlier":"Staff had brought the results of commission review and full-scale mockups. The committee was being asked how to proceed with the designs.","response":"Gordo later said he was not ready even to narrow the process and asked for more options.","outcome":"The committee did not select a design. Madison also described the temporary solution as inelegant but effective, which he thought allowed time for further consideration.","links":[{"label":"Staff report and advisory feedback","source":"r2021"},{"label":"Temporary-fence comment","source":"v2021","time":"01:08:59"},{"label":"Gordo’s response on narrowing","source":"v2021","time":"01:21:36"}],"refs":"9, 11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-staffing-2021","title":"The practical limits of intervention by personnel","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:36:11","topic":"staffing","quote":"the efficacy of it is really hard to to predict","context":"Madison said he had discussed staffing with residents and staff. He questioned whether personnel would be in the right place and able to act when needed.","earlier":"Hampton had asked about continuous staffing, Mermell had responded, and Gordo had proposed a host or guide model.","response":"Madison identified the bridge’s length, curvature, two sides, and varied incident timing. The discussion also raised the limits of physical intervention and liability.","outcome":"The same practical issue returned when Madison described rapidly unfolding incidents in November 2023. This was a discussion of feasibility, not a measured trial of staffing at this bridge.","links":[{"label":"Gordo’s preceding proposal","source":"v2021","time":"01:31:59"},{"label":"Later response-time discussion","source":"v2023","time":"00:52:29"}],"refs":"11, 15","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-safety","title":"Safety and the bridge’s appearance","date":"November 15, 2023","time":"00:29:07","meeting":"v2023","kind":"Quotation","quote":"elevate human safety over aesthetics and objects","context":"Madison said the proposed designs would change the bridge’s appearance. He also said that a solution achieving both safety and preservation goals would be more successful.","outcome":"He later asked about netting and landscaping and was not ready to support one of the three designs.","basis":"Quoted in the retained research account. The timestamp is the approximate start of the surrounding passage.","refs":"14","topic":"design","earlier":"The Council had directed a vertical-barrier design process in April 2018. A first design cycle and full-scale mockups preceded the three new concepts presented in 2023.","response":"Staff described rescue, maintenance, and preservation concerns associated with horizontal netting. The later exchange addressed landscaping and the bridge’s geometry.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Council decision","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Staff and consultant responses","source":"v2023","time":"00:29:29"},{"label":"Three proposed concepts","source":"p2023"}],"sortDate":"2023-11-15","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-trees","title":"Returning to trees and shrubs below the bridge","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:33:24","topic":"landscaping","quote":"have we looked at even solutions as simple as, you know, thick trees and shrubs and bushes that would then break a fall?","context":"Madison asked about landscaping as a cushioning or deterrent measure while exploring a combination of approaches.","earlier":"The April 2018 report said no verifying scientific data had been found for that role. About one-fifth of the span, including its highest point, could not be planted. It treated trees as a possible supplement.","response":"Markarian said the task force had examined landscaping and researched measures elsewhere. Staff and the consultant discussed the local geometry, remaining fall distance, structural demands, and appearance of netting.","outcome":"Madison continued to favor studying a combined approach and remained unwilling to support the three proposed designs.","links":[{"label":"Earlier landscaping finding","source":"r2018","page":3},{"label":"2018 presentation of planting limits","source":"v2018","time":"01:04:17"},{"label":"Staff’s response","source":"v2023","time":"00:33:47"},{"label":"Later position on the designs","source":"v2023","time":"00:40:13"}],"refs":"5, 14","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-not-ready","title":"Declining to support the three proposed designs","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:40:13","topic":"design","quote":"I don’t think I could support any of these options","context":"After the discussion of netting and landscaping, Madison said a substantial change in community sentiment would be needed for him to support one of the designs. He had suggested the temporary fence might be preferable.","earlier":"Staff had explained the prior alternatives work and site-specific constraints. The new design cycle had brought three concepts.","response":"Olmos proposed completing commission review, presenting survey results, and returning with additional information about netting and other questions.","outcome":"This was an explicit statement about the presented designs. It does not establish that Madison rejected every research finding discussed at the meeting.","links":[{"label":"Preceding alternatives exchange","source":"v2023","time":"00:33:24"},{"label":"Jones on continuing the process","source":"v2023","time":"00:55:35"},{"label":"Staff’s closing proposal","source":"v2023","time":"01:02:09"}],"refs":"14","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-response","title":"Could stationed personnel respond in time?","date":"November 15, 2023","time":"00:52:29","meeting":"v2023","kind":"Quotation","quote":"unfold in five minutes","context":"Madison used this phrase while questioning whether personnel stationed along the bridge could reach an incident quickly enough. He had also discussed how capital funds might support an ongoing staffing expense.","outcome":"The committee requested more staffing analysis. Staff treated personnel as a possible supplement to a physical barrier.","basis":"Quotation retained in the research account. The author checked this exchange’s speaker handoffs and locators against the video. The displayed time starts Madison’s passage, not the exact quoted phrase.","refs":"15","topic":"staffing","earlier":"Madison had raised the bridge’s length, curvature, and unpredictable incident timing during the August 2021 staffing discussion. Markarian had just repeated coverage and liability concerns in 2023.","response":"Gordo acknowledged the response-time concern but asked whether the presence of personnel could deter an impulsive attempt. Olmos described patrol as a complementary measure.","links":[{"label":"Madison’s earlier practical concerns","source":"v2021","time":"01:36:11"},{"label":"Markarian’s staffing response","source":"v2023","time":"00:47:01"},{"label":"Gordo follows up","source":"v2023","time":"00:53:38"}],"sortDate":"2023-11-15","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-health-expertise","title":"Asking for Public Health at the next discussion","meeting":"v2023","time":"01:01:34","topic":"effectiveness","quote":"public health needs to be involved in this discussion","context":"Madison called for health expertise after Olmos and Markarian explained that a barrier can buy time for a crisis to ease or responders to arrive.","earlier":"Public Health and mental-health participants had been involved in the earlier process. Jones had just asked about their role and substitution.","response":"Staff said they had consulted people in the field and proposed bringing health professionals with the survey results and follow-up information.","outcome":"The July 2024 meeting included Sandri Kramer of Didi Hirsch, who explained means restriction and the importance of time for intervention.","links":[{"label":"Jones’s preceding question","source":"v2023","time":"00:56:47"},{"label":"Barrier buys time exchange","source":"v2023","time":"01:00:41"},{"label":"Markarian on time for responders","source":"v2023","time":"01:01:26"},{"label":"Kramer’s July discussion","source":"v2024","time":"00:42:08"}],"refs":"14, 18–19","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-life","title":"Human life and safety","date":"July 17, 2024","time":"01:03:10","meeting":"v2024","kind":"Transcript excerpt","quote":"we have to elevate human life and human safety over really objects of any kind","context":"Madison expressed sympathy to a family and recalled why the emergency fence had been installed. He remained uncertain that the proposed permanent designs had sufficiently changed.","outcome":"He later asked about ground cover and layered netting. No permanent design was sent to the full Council at this meeting.","basis":"Wording from the preserved working transcript. The approximate player locator is retained in the reviewed source note. This website update did not add a new word-for-word listening check.","refs":"18, 22","topic":"design","earlier":"The July presentation returned with patrol costs, technology analysis, netting and rescue review, Golden Gate consultation, survey results, and prevention expertise requested during earlier discussions.","response":"Madison also acknowledged public-safety concerns about netting while asking whether the City had exhausted alternatives.","links":[{"label":"Material returned to the committee","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Later alternatives exchange","source":"v2024","time":"01:06:24"}],"sortDate":"2024-07-17","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-ground-cover","title":"Have all the options been exhausted?","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:06:35","topic":"landscaping","quote":"Do you feel like we’ve really done our level best to exhaust all the feasible options?","context":"At 01:06:24, Madison said leadership sometimes requires proceeding without a great option. At 01:06:35, he asked Markarian whether the City had exhausted the feasible options. The broader discussion included ground cover, combined measures, and consultation with Caltech.","earlier":"Landscaping had been addressed by the 2018 task force and again in November 2023. The July meeting returned with the requested wider engineering and rescue review.","response":"Beginning at 01:06:45, Markarian described more than seven years on the project and experts with different perspectives. She said, “we have truly exhausted various mitigation measures,” then discussed the Golden Gate comparison. In the later ground-cover exchange, she explained why the fall distance limited measures at ground level.","outcome":"Madison continued to ask about combined measures and then a layered-net concept. The proposals were related to earlier questions but were not identical.","links":[{"label":"Leadership prelude and context","source":"v2024","time":"01:06:24"},{"label":"2018 landscaping finding","source":"r2018","page":3},{"label":"November 2023 question","source":"v2023","time":"00:33:24"},{"label":"Markarian describes the work","source":"v2024","time":"01:06:45"},{"label":"Ground-level response","source":"v2024","time":"01:10:37"},{"label":"Layered-net follow-up","source":"v2024","time":"01:11:27"}],"refs":"5, 14, 18, 22","kind":"Author-checked quotation","basis":"The author manually checked the official recording on September 22, 2026, confirming the question’s wording, including “feasible,” and the locators: leadership prelude at 01:06:24, question at 01:06:35, and Markarian’s answer at 01:06:45. The last time marks the start of her answer, not the exact instant of her quoted phrase. This check does not cover the whole meeting.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"madison-layered-nets","title":"Asking about layered netting and its effectiveness","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:11:27","topic":"netting","quote":"I think my question was more about the efficacy and whether it was considered","context":"Madison proposed more than one layer of netting with ground cover. After hearing about cost and engineering complexity, he said his question concerned effectiveness and whether the idea had been considered.","earlier":"The committee had received the site-specific review of a horizontal net and Fire’s rescue concerns. Earlier landscaping findings did not evaluate this exact layered configuration.","response":"Markarian described the added structural reach. The architect described cost, appearance, and engineering complexity and said the concept was not impossible.","outcome":"The exchange supplied further design information but did not establish a feasible or approved system. No permanent design was sent to the full Council.","links":[{"label":"Question about layers","source":"v2024","time":"01:11:27"},{"label":"Staff and architect responses","source":"v2024","time":"01:12:18"},{"label":"Madison’s quoted follow-up","source":"v2024","time":"01:13:35"},{"label":"July presentation","source":"p2024"}],"refs":"16, 18, 22","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"gordo":{"name":"Victor Gordo","remarks":[{"id":"gordo-2018-regional","title":"Would deaths fall across the region or move elsewhere?","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:31:15","topic":"effectiveness","quote":null,"context":"Gordo asked whether a bridge barrier would reduce deaths across the region or simply change where they occurred. He then asked about the supporting study.","earlier":"Wilson had raised substitution earlier in the same discussion. Public Health Director Michael Johnson had begun explaining the research.","response":"Johnson said the research showed an overall decline after barriers were installed and referred to a Toronto study.","outcome":"The Council proceeded with the task-force recommendation. Similar substitution questions returned in 2023. Successive studies and different outcomes still require separate interpretation.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Public Health answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"},{"label":"Johnson answers Gordo","source":"v2018","time":"01:31:51"},{"label":"Gordo asks about the study","source":"v2018","time":"01:32:08"},{"label":"Johnson resumes","source":"v2018","time":"01:32:15"}],"refs":"7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the recorded discussion retained in the sent research account and source note. The player locators are inherited at their documented scope. This is not a verbatim quotation or a new listening check.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"gordo-decoupling","title":"The temporary fence and the bridge’s reputation","date":"August 18, 2021","time":"01:21:22","meeting":"v2021","kind":"Quotation","quote":"the decoupling, if you will, has started","context":"Gordo picked up Madison’s discussion of place and method. He said the temporary structure appeared to be discouraging people from using the bridge to harm themselves.","outcome":"He also said the committee was not ready to narrow the permanent designs and called for more options and public input.","basis":"Quoted wording and locator retained in the reviewed account of the official recording. This is Gordo’s interpretation of the local record, not a new causal estimate.","refs":"11","topic":"effectiveness","earlier":"Madison had discussed the relationship between a particular place, an immediate crisis, and access to a method. The temporary fence had been installed in 2018.","response":"Gordo interpreted the local record as evidence of deterrence while saying the work was incomplete. His statement is an interpretation of descriptive local counts, not an independently established causal result.","links":[{"label":"Madison’s earlier discussion","source":"v2021","time":"01:03:49"},{"label":"Gordo on narrowing the designs","source":"v2021","time":"01:21:36"}],"sortDate":"2021-08-18","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-more-options","title":"More options and another round of public input","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:21:36","topic":"design","quote":"we ought to send this back for more community input","context":"Gordo said he was not ready to narrow the permanent designs. He discussed different materials and further consideration of the enclosure concept.","earlier":"The 2018 policy direction, the first design cycle, and commission review preceded this request. Madison had proposed reducing the field to two choices.","response":"The enclosure study had already produced additional design information. Gordo’s discussion concerned both materials and the opportunity for community input.","outcome":"Further design work and input followed. The repeated “get it right” phrases appeared within these specific requests.","links":[{"label":"Earlier design comparison","source":"v2021","time":"00:48:33"},{"label":"More-options request","source":"v2021","time":"01:22:23"},{"label":"Later community-input passage","source":"v2021","time":"01:23:41"},{"label":"Staff’s closing response","source":"v2021","time":"01:43:48"}],"refs":"9, 11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-host-program","title":"A host or guide model for the bridge","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:31:59","topic":"staffing","quote":"I don’t think we should dismiss outright the concept of achieving our goal of suicide prevention by manning the bridge","context":"Gordo drew on the Old Pasadena host or guide program and suggested personnel who could observe, report, and offer historical information.","earlier":"Mermell had just said staffing was previously suggested and questioned its feasibility as the solution.","response":"Madison discussed visibility, the size of the bridge, incident timing, and whether personnel should physically intervene.","outcome":"Gordo later clarified that he also had a hybrid of a physical measure and staffing in mind. A program used in Old Pasadena did not itself establish effectiveness for bridge suicide prevention.","links":[{"label":"Mermell’s preceding answer","source":"v2021","time":"01:27:11"},{"label":"Madison’s response","source":"v2021","time":"01:36:11"},{"label":"Gordo’s hybrid clarification","source":"v2021","time":"01:45:08"}],"refs":"11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-hybrid","title":"Clarifying a combined physical and staffing approach","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:45:08","topic":"staffing","quote":"maybe it’s a hybrid","context":"Gordo clarified that his interest in staffing could involve combining it with a physical design and the more transparent material shown that evening.","earlier":"Staff and committee members had discussed coverage, intervention, cost, and liability concerns.","response":"Mermell’s closing summary called for consultation with the design team and a further timetable. He said a three-month return was probably too aggressive.","outcome":"The clarification matters because the staffing discussion included both a possible alternative and a possible complement to physical protection.","links":[{"label":"Staff’s timetable response","source":"v2021","time":"01:43:48"},{"label":"Earlier staffing discussion","source":"v2021","time":"01:31:59"}],"refs":"11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-staffing","title":"Revisiting a staffing model","date":"November 15, 2023","time":"00:51:52","meeting":"v2023","kind":"Quotation","quote":"money well spent","context":"Gordo suggested that people affected by suicidal crises might view a staffing expense of “a couple hundred thousand dollars” this way. He asked staff to revisit the model and return with recommendations.","outcome":"Markarian had explained cost, coverage, liability, and off-hours concerns. Gordo later requested mental-health input on whether a visible human presence could deter an impulsive attempt.","basis":"Quoted fragments and the passage locator are retained in the source note. The author checked the speaker handoffs and times against the official video. This oral amount was not an approved staffing budget.","refs":"15","topic":"staffing","earlier":"Gordo had proposed a guide or host model in August 2021. In this meeting, Markarian again described continuous coverage, multiple personnel, liability, and incidents outside ordinary working hours.","response":"Gordo asked staff to return with recommendations despite those concerns. The later discussion separated immediate response from a possible deterrent effect of human presence.","links":[{"label":"Gordo’s 2021 proposal","source":"v2021","time":"01:31:59"},{"label":"Markarian’s earlier answer that evening","source":"v2023","time":"00:47:01"},{"label":"Follow-up about mental-health advice","source":"v2023","time":"00:53:38"}],"sortDate":"2023-11-15","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-presence","title":"Requesting expert advice on the deterrent effect of human presence","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:53:38","topic":"staffing","quote":"I would ask that staff talk with professionals, psychologists and psychiatrists to see if in fact that might make a difference","context":"Gordo distinguished an immediate response from the possibility that a visible human presence might prevent an impulsive attempt from beginning. He asked for professional advice.","earlier":"Markarian had explained the earlier staffing assessment. Madison had just questioned whether personnel could respond quickly enough.","response":"Olmos said patrol and technology could enhance a barrier and asked whether to continue the design process or pause.","outcome":"Commission review and the survey were to continue, with additional responses and health expertise when the project returned.","links":[{"label":"Staff’s earlier staffing assessment","source":"v2023","time":"00:47:01"},{"label":"Madison’s response-time concern","source":"v2023","time":"00:52:29"},{"label":"Olmos asks for direction","source":"v2023","time":"00:54:37"},{"label":"Next steps","source":"v2023","time":"01:02:09"}],"refs":"14–15","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-rescue-clarification","title":"Clarifying which design raised Fire’s rescue concerns","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:08:36","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Gordo asked whether Fire’s concern about risks to people and responders applied to vertical mesh.","earlier":"Fire’s earlier presentation had discussed the difficulties of rescues involving a horizontal net.","response":"Fire clarified that the concern was about horizontal netting and explicitly supported a vertical barrier. Gordo acknowledged the clarification.","outcome":"This exchange helps distinguish opposition to a particular local net design from opposition to physical protection generally.","links":[{"label":"Fire’s earlier assessment","source":"v2024","time":"00:34:00"},{"label":"Fire’s clarification","source":"v2024","time":"01:09:16"}],"refs":"18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the preserved City-caption exchange, approximately 01:08:36 to 01:09:40, with the speaker handoff naming the mayor. These are caption-derived passage locators, not newly verified audio boundaries.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"gordo-remote-intervention","title":"Requesting a closer look at remote intervention","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:14:21","topic":"technology","quote":"I’d ask the experts to look at that technology","context":"Gordo described monitored cameras with speakers that could allow a trained person to engage someone on the bridge in real time.","earlier":"Technology and staffing had been studied previously and revisited after the November 2023 discussion. Hampton had also just suggested cameras and motion detection.","response":"In the following exchange with Jones, Markarian described such measures as complements, discussed operational limits, and questioned their effectiveness alone.","outcome":"Gordo offered to pass along information. This was a request to examine an application of technology, not a demonstrated substitute for physical protection.","links":[{"label":"Hampton’s earlier suggestion","source":"v2024","time":"01:00:30"},{"label":"Technology review","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Staff response during Jones’s questions","source":"v2024","time":"01:16:54"}],"refs":"16, 18","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"hampton":{"name":"Tyron Hampton","remarks":[{"id":"hampton-2018-action","sortOrder":2,"title":"Supporting the barrier direction and interim fencing","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:39:51","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"The official minutes record Hampton’s request to consider temporary fencing at the bridge’s ends and approaches until an approved design could be built. He also seconded the staff recommendation.","earlier":"The task force recommended physical protection while treating other measures as possible supplements.","response":"The Council unanimously approved the recommendation moved by Madison.","outcome":"Hampton’s support for interim protection and the permanent direction forms part of the earlier record.","links":[{"label":"Hampton’s request and the Council action","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Task-force recommendation","source":"r2018"}],"refs":"5–7","kind":"Recorded action","basis":"The request and second are documented in the official Council minutes, page 5. The displayed recording time locates the motion-and-approval sequence only. It is not an exact timestamp for Hampton’s separate temporary-fencing request.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"hampton-right","title":"Further consideration in 2021","date":"August 18, 2021","time":"01:26:03","meeting":"v2021","kind":"Quotation","quote":"get this right","context":"Hampton said the temporary structure was working and continued considering alternatives for a permanent approach. His proposal for around-the-clock staffing followed at 01:26:44.","outcome":"Mermell responded that staffing had been considered and was not feasible as a replacement. Further examination of a complementary staffing role remained in the discussion.","basis":"Quoted phrase and locator retained in the reviewed source note. The phrase alone does not establish that the requested consideration was redundant.","refs":"11","topic":"design","earlier":"The meeting followed commission review and full-scale mockups. Staff had also developed an enclosure concept in response to earlier direction.","response":"Hampton praised staff’s work, supported further consideration, and asked whether an enclosure could take a form more compatible with the historic bridge.","links":[{"label":"Refined design-goal comparison","source":"v2021","time":"00:48:33"},{"label":"Staff’s closing summary","source":"v2021","time":"01:43:48"}],"sortDate":"2021-08-18","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-24-hours","title":"Asking whether the bridge could be staffed around the clock","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:26:44","topic":"staffing","quote":"Has that ever even come up?","context":"Hampton asked whether staffing the bridge for 24 hours had been considered and what two-person coverage would cost.","earlier":"The task force had considered complementary measures. The committee was discussing a permanent design after the mockup reviews.","response":"Mermell said staffing had been suggested before, gave a rough annual cost in the hundreds of thousands, and questioned its feasibility. He favored retaining the temporary fence until another solution was ready.","outcome":"Gordo then developed a host or guide model, while Madison raised practical intervention concerns. No staffing program was approved in this exchange.","links":[{"label":"Mermell’s response","source":"v2021","time":"01:27:11"},{"label":"Gordo’s guide proposal","source":"v2021","time":"01:31:59"},{"label":"Madison’s practical concerns","source":"v2021","time":"01:36:11"}],"refs":"11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-urgency","title":"Asking for a faster return","date":"July 17, 2024","time":"00:58:57","meeting":"v2024","kind":"Transcript excerpt","quote":"I think that time is of the essence.","context":"Hampton asked for direction and a return to the committee in roughly 90 days rather than early the following year.","outcome":"He named vertical mesh as his preferred design at 00:59:36 and also raised cameras and motion detection as possible supplements.","basis":"Wording from the preserved working transcript. The player locator and the substance of the request are retained in the reviewed source note and minutes. No new word-for-word listening check was performed for this website.","refs":"20","topic":"design","earlier":"The proposed schedule anticipated further design work, public feedback, commission review, and a return to Public Safety early in 2025.","response":"Hampton asked for a shorter return interval and gave a design preference. His requested 90 days was not a completed milestone or a construction deadline.","links":[{"label":"Vertical mesh preference","source":"v2024","time":"00:59:36"},{"label":"Meeting minutes","source":"m2024"}],"sortDate":"2024-07-17","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-mesh","title":"Naming vertical mesh as a preferred design","meeting":"v2024","time":"00:59:36","topic":"design","quote":"I think the vertical mesh is the most ideal","context":"Hampton identified vertical mesh as his preference and discussed its appearance and the bridge’s public identity.","earlier":"Staff’s remaining concepts were vertical webmesh and metal pickets. The committee also had the fuller netting, patrol, and prevention review.","response":"Hampton accompanied his preference with a request for a faster return and possible camera and motion-detection measures.","outcome":"Later in the meeting, he still asked to keep horizontal netting in consideration. Both statements belong in the record.","links":[{"label":"Request for a faster return","source":"v2024","time":"00:58:57"},{"label":"Technology suggestion","source":"v2024","time":"01:00:30"},{"label":"Later position on netting","source":"v2024","time":"01:21:29"}],"refs":"16, 20–21","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-cameras","title":"Cameras and motion detection as added protection","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:00:30","topic":"technology","quote":"we need to put cameras up on the bridge with motion detectors","context":"Hampton proposed camera and sensor alerts to Police as an addition to the vertical mesh he had just preferred.","earlier":"The task force had considered technology, and the July presentation returned with further technology analysis.","response":"The meeting’s later staff response treated technology as a potential complement to physical protection and raised operational concerns.","outcome":"This was a suggestion for additional protection, not proof of an approved camera system or its measured effectiveness.","links":[{"label":"Technology and other measures reviewed","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Later staff response on complementary tools","source":"v2024","time":"01:16:54"}],"refs":"16, 18, 20","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-patrol-again","title":"Returning to netting and continuous patrol","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:19:40","topic":"staffing","quote":"I still think that those are factors that we should put out","context":"Hampton said he had raised horizontal netting and continuous patrol at the previous meeting and still wanted them considered, while acknowledging cost.","earlier":"Jones had just asked about patrol and the netting recommendation. Markarian had given the reasons against patrol as a substitute and said the netting recommendation was unanimous.","response":"Hampton moved on to ask Fire about rescue cushions and whether a higher-capacity version existed.","outcome":"He retained an interest in these measures alongside his earlier preference for vertical mesh and call to move forward.","links":[{"label":"Patrol answer","source":"v2024","time":"01:16:54"},{"label":"Netting answer","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:41"},{"label":"Rescue-cushion exchange","source":"v2024","time":"01:20:04"}],"refs":"18, 20–21","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-cushion","title":"A higher-capacity rescue cushion: a new unanswered question","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:20:55","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"After Fire described the limits of its rescue cushion, Hampton asked whether equipment rated for a greater height existed.","earlier":"Fire had said its cushion was rated for roughly 100 feet, while portions of the bridge reached roughly 150 feet, with positioning constraints below.","response":"Fire did not know whether a higher-capacity cushion was available and agreed to investigate.","outcome":"This was a question the City had not already answered. A May 2026 presentation listed a rescue cushion without a capacity rating, so it did not resolve the question.","links":[{"label":"Initial cushion question and Fire response","source":"v2024","time":"01:20:04"},{"label":"Higher-capacity question and response","source":"v2024","time":"01:20:55"}],"refs":"21","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the recorded discussion retained in the sent research account and source note. The player locators are inherited at their documented scope. This is not a verbatim quotation or a new listening check. The displayed time marks the surrounding follow-up, not an exact utterance boundary. The May 2026 record status is inherited from the retained source note, not a new equipment check.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"hampton-netting","title":"Keeping horizontal netting in consideration","date":"July 17, 2024","time":"01:21:29","meeting":"v2024","kind":"Transcript excerpt","quote":"the horizontal wouldn’t be an idea I’d tell you to completely cancel out","context":"Later in the same meeting, Hampton continued to favor considering horizontal netting after staff’s recommendation against it and Fire’s explanation of rescue concerns. He described it as the most attractive-looking option.","outcome":"He had also asked whether a higher-capacity rescue cushion existed. Fire did not know and agreed to investigate. That question was distinct from his preference for vertical mesh earlier in the meeting.","basis":"Wording from the preserved working transcript, with typographic apostrophes. The approximate player locator is retained in the reviewed source note. No new word-for-word listening check was performed for this website.","refs":"20–21","topic":"netting","earlier":"The expert review had not recommended horizontal netting. Fire had explained rescue and resource concerns, and staff had just repeated that the recommendation was unanimous.","response":"Hampton continued to favor keeping netting under consideration. He also said the project needed to move forward. The higher-capacity rescue-cushion question remained unanswered.","links":[{"label":"Fire’s rescue assessment","source":"v2024","time":"00:34:00"},{"label":"Unanimous recommendation clarified","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:41"},{"label":"Rescue-cushion exchange","source":"v2024","time":"01:20:04"}],"sortDate":"2024-07-17","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"jones":{"name":"Justin Jones","remarks":[{"id":"jones-other-options","title":"Asking what other options had been considered","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:41:34","topic":"design","quote":"Are there any other options that the working group looked into that you could share with us?","context":"Jones relayed residents’ concerns about both the existing fence and the proposed permanent designs and asked about other options.","earlier":"The Council had selected a vertical-barrier direction in 2018. Jones was not part of that decision or the 2021 committee discussion.","response":"Olmos explained that developing barrier designs was staff’s direction and that the other options had already gone through extensive discussion. Staff could study alternatives again if directed.","outcome":"Jones asked about the working group, costs, and survey and later supported continuing commission review.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Council direction","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Olmos’s response","source":"v2023","time":"00:41:40"},{"label":"Jones on the next steps","source":"v2023","time":"00:55:35"}],"refs":"6, 12, 14","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The displayed time is an approximate passage locator. No new listening check was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"jones-continue","title":"Continuing commission review and the survey","date":"November 15, 2023","time":"00:55:35","meeting":"v2023","kind":"Caption excerpt","quote":"continue to go to the commissions and then get the results of the survey","context":"After Olmos asked whether staff should continue or pause, Jones recommended proceeding with commission review and hearing the survey results.","outcome":"Jones also asked about Public Health involvement and whether restricting access at one site shifted attempts elsewhere. Staff agreed to bring health professionals when the project returned.","basis":"Wording from the preserved City captions. The author’s completed recording check covers the speaker, passage, and approximate player locator. Caption cue starts differ slightly from that player locator.","refs":"14","topic":"design","earlier":"Olmos had asked whether to continue the planned process or pause to evaluate complementary measures. Staff had explained that the Council’s earlier direction was to develop vertical barriers.","response":"Jones supported continuing to the commissions and returning with the survey. Olmos’s closing proposal also included responses to the committee’s questions and health expertise.","links":[{"label":"Olmos asks for direction","source":"v2023","time":"00:54:37"},{"label":"Olmos’s proposed next steps","source":"v2023","time":"01:02:09"}],"sortDate":"2023-11-15","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"jones-public-health","title":"Public Health involvement and substitution","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:56:47","topic":"effectiveness","quote":null,"context":"Jones asked how Public Health had participated and whether blocking access at one bridge could shift attempts to another place. He also raised a broader citywide prevention approach.","earlier":"The Council had discussed substitution with Public Health in April 2018. That earlier answer concerned research, including a Toronto study.","response":"Olmos relayed the mental-health specialist’s explanation that interrupting access to a selected bridge would not simply send everyone to the next bridge. Staff agreed to bring health professionals when the project returned.","outcome":"The question recurred, but Jones was newer to the discussion. The staff explanation is distinct from a claim that every study finds no displacement at every location.","links":[{"label":"2018 Public Health discussion","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"},{"label":"Gordo’s 2018 follow-up","source":"v2018","time":"01:31:15"},{"label":"Olmos’s answer to Jones","source":"v2023","time":"00:57:36"},{"label":"Plan to bring health expertise","source":"v2023","time":"01:02:09"}],"refs":"7, 12, 14","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the recorded discussion retained in the sent research account and source note. The player locators are inherited at their documented scope. This is not a verbatim quotation or a new listening check.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"jones-patrol","title":"Patrol: cost, effectiveness, or both?","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:16:54","topic":"staffing","quote":null,"context":"Jones asked whether staff’s recommendation against continuous patrol was based on its cost or its effectiveness.","earlier":"The committee had requested further staffing analysis in November 2023. The July presentation estimated roughly $600,000 annually for private security and $1.16 million for law-enforcement coverage.","response":"Markarian said both cost and effectiveness were concerns, along with liability and training. She distinguished measures that might help from a replacement for physical protection.","outcome":"Jones then clarified the netting recommendation and supported pursuing one of the two remaining designs.","links":[{"label":"November 2023 request","source":"v2023","time":"00:51:52"},{"label":"Returned cost estimates","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Staff’s answer","source":"v2024","time":"01:17:12"},{"label":"Jones’s design position","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:55"}],"refs":"15–16, 18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the recorded discussion retained in the sent research account and source note. The player locators are inherited at their documented scope. This is not a verbatim quotation or a new listening check.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"jones-netting-review","title":"Was any expert recommending horizontal netting?","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:18:41","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Jones asked whether any expert or task-force participant was recommending horizontal netting.","earlier":"The July meeting had presented the expanded engineering and rescue review. Fire had explained why it could not recommend a net from a public-safety standpoint.","response":"Markarian answered that horizontal netting was unanimously not recommended.","outcome":"Jones accepted that clarification in the exchange and expressed support for pursuing one of the two remaining designs. This does not establish that nets are ineffective at other bridges.","links":[{"label":"Fire’s recommendation","source":"v2024","time":"00:35:48"},{"label":"Markarian’s answer","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:50"},{"label":"Jones supports proceeding","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:55"}],"refs":"16, 18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary of the recorded discussion retained in the sent research account and source note. The player locators are inherited at their documented scope. This is not a verbatim quotation or a new listening check.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"jones-design","title":"Pursuing one of the two remaining designs","date":"July 17, 2024","time":"01:18:55","meeting":"v2024","kind":"Author-confirmed excerpt","quote":"I would be in favor of moving forward with pursuing one of these two options and being able to develop a plan and come back to us.","context":"Jones had asked whether patrol was ruled out because of cost, effectiveness, or both, and whether any expert had recommended horizontal netting. Staff answered both on patrol and said the recommendation against netting was unanimous.","outcome":"He supported pursuing a design and returning with further refinement. This was not final approval or selection of a particular design.","basis":"The author confirmed the highlighted 01:18:55–01:19:30 passage against the official recording. This excerpt is a continuous portion of the checked text. The timestamp is an approximate passage locator.","refs":"18","topic":"design","earlier":"Jones had just clarified the reasons for rejecting patrol as a substitute and asked whether any expert had recommended netting. Both vertical webmesh and metal pickets remained under consideration.","response":"He proposed using the feedback to refine components and variations and return with a plan. His request for refinement accompanied support for proceeding.","links":[{"label":"Patrol clarification","source":"v2024","time":"01:16:54"},{"label":"Netting clarification","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:41"},{"label":"Meeting minutes","source":"m2024"}],"sortDate":"2024-07-17","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]}};
const otherSpeakers={"delgado":{"name":"Julianna Delgado","role":"Design Commission chair","remarks":[{"id":"delgado-alternatives","title":"Asking about horizontal and landscape alternatives","meeting":"vjan24","time":"00:41:27","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Delgado acknowledged the staff work and asked whether the team had considered horizontal protection or landscaping. She compared the discussion with the Golden Gate Bridge.","earlier":"The 2018 task force had compared netting, landscaping, and vertical barriers. November 2023 staff discussion had again addressed local netting constraints.","response":"An off-camera respondent at 00:42:33 described the earlier alternatives review and the Council’s vertical-barrier direction. Research-preference wording at 00:43:59 is author-confirmed, but the respondent’s identity and role remain unresolved.","outcome":"The commission did not reach consensus on the three designs. This exchange establishes a renewed alternatives question, not that the respondent had refused to conduct research.","links":[{"label":"Earlier alternatives comparison","source":"p2018","page":29},{"label":"Earlier netting discussion","source":"v2023","time":"00:33:24"},{"label":"Off-camera response","source":"vjan24","time":"00:42:33"},{"label":"Research-preference wording","source":"vjan24","time":"00:43:59"},{"label":"Commission minutes","source":"mjan24"}],"refs":"5, 14, 33","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-01-09","date":"January 9, 2024","body":"Design Commission"},{"id":"delgado-previous-council","title":"Questioning the earlier Council direction","meeting":"vjan24","time":"00:45:34","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Delgado referred to the “previous City Council” while questioning the restriction of the current design work to vertical barriers.","earlier":"The Council adopted the task-force recommendation in April 2018. Its policy choice and the later selection of a particular design were separate decisions.","response":"The exchange returned to whether earlier direction should constrain the options now under review. The off-camera respondent’s name and role are not established.","outcome":"No revised Council policy resulted from this commission discussion. Its minutes record no consensus on the designs.","links":[{"label":"Adopted Council direction","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Commission minutes","source":"mjan24"}],"refs":"6, 33","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-01-09","date":"January 9, 2024","body":"Design Commission"},{"id":"delgado-cacti","title":"Suggesting cacti and relaying a housing suggestion","meeting":"vjan24","time":"00:47:03","topic":"landscaping","quote":"It could be cacti below it.","context":"Delgado suggested cacti below the bridge and relayed a public suggestion for an apartment building below it. These were suggestions made during design discussion.","earlier":"The task-force report had found no verifying data for trees as a cushion or deterrent and identified portions of the bridge where planting was not possible. It allowed a possible supplemental role.","response":"A respondent discussed existing bungalows at 00:47:29. The later response, around 00:47:50, returned to the safety reason for keeping people on the bridge deck. The respondent remains unidentified.","outcome":"Neither cacti nor housing became an adopted protection measure in this meeting. The earlier trees review does not experimentally test every later landscape suggestion.","links":[{"label":"Earlier landscaping finding","source":"r2018","page":3},{"label":"Existing-building response","source":"vjan24","time":"00:47:29"},{"label":"Deck-safety response","source":"vjan24","time":"00:47:50"}],"refs":"5, 33","kind":"Working-transcript excerpt","basis":"Short continuous excerpt from the preserved January 9 focused transcript and speaker record, which includes earlier listening notes. The retained source note supplies the approximate player locator. No new listening was performed. The responding voice is not identified as staff or a consultant.","sortDate":"2024-01-09","date":"January 9, 2024","body":"Design Commission"},{"id":"delgado-three-designs","title":"Objecting to all three presented designs","meeting":"vjan24","time":"01:06:28","topic":"design","quote":"I’m having a problem too with all three of these solutions","context":"Delgado said she was having a problem with all three solutions and discussed their materials, appearance, and relationship to the bridge.","earlier":"The replacement design team had developed three concepts through the 2023 review process. The Council’s original safety direction remained separate from design approval.","response":"Her objection concerned the presented solutions. It does not establish rejection of every finding about suicide prevention.","outcome":"The Design Commission reached no consensus. The project returned to further review rather than receiving a final design approval.","links":[{"label":"Design memo","source":"jan24memo"},{"label":"Commission minutes","source":"mjan24"}],"refs":"13, 33","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The time is an approximate passage locator. Caption cue starts can differ from the retained player locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-01-09","date":"January 9, 2024","body":"Design Commission"}]},"kennedy":{"name":"John J. Kennedy","role":"Councilmember","remarks":[{"id":"kennedy-review","title":"Narrowing the options and seeking local expertise","date":"August 18, 2021","time":"01:16:41","meeting":"v2021","kind":"Quotation","quote":"one last time out","context":"Kennedy supported narrowing the options and placing the project on a timetable. He also supported another opportunity for local expertise to contribute to the two-option approach Madison had discussed.","outcome":"The committee did not select a permanent design. More design work and community input followed.","basis":"Quoted phrase and locator retained in the reviewed source note. The brief excerpt is presented with Kennedy’s support for narrowing and a timetable, rather than as a complete statement of his position.","refs":"9, 11","sortDate":"2021-08-18","body":"Public Safety Committee","topic":"design","earlier":"The Council’s 2018 direction had been followed by commission review and full-scale mockups. The committee was now considering which designs should proceed.","response":"Madison had proposed narrowing the field. Mermell also explained the cost and coverage concerns about staffing as a replacement for a barrier.","links":[{"label":"Staff report and advisory feedback","source":"r2021"},{"label":"Madison on narrowing","source":"v2021","time":"01:08:35"},{"label":"Mermell on staffing","source":"v2021","time":"01:27:11"}]},{"id":"kennedy-staffing","title":"Staffing, liability, and further review","date":"August 18, 2021","time":"01:41:11","meeting":"v2021","kind":"Quotation","quote":"get it right","context":"Kennedy’s preceding discussion, beginning at 01:39:01, raised liability and intervention concerns about staffing. He acknowledged the effort to save lives while questioning how personnel could safely intervene.","outcome":"He called for another round of consideration. Earlier in the meeting, he had been ready to move away from his enclosure concept and narrow the field to vertical-barrier options.","basis":"Quoted phrase and approximate locator retained in the reviewed source note. These selected remarks do not identify a private motive or measure how much delay any individual caused.","refs":"9, 11","sortDate":"2021-08-18","body":"Public Safety Committee","topic":"staffing","earlier":"The Council’s 2018 direction had been followed by commission review and full-scale mockups. The committee was now considering which designs should proceed.","response":"Madison had proposed narrowing the field. Mermell also explained the cost and coverage concerns about staffing as a replacement for a barrier.","links":[{"label":"Staff report and advisory feedback","source":"r2021"},{"label":"Madison on narrowing","source":"v2021","time":"01:08:35"},{"label":"Mermell on staffing","source":"v2021","time":"01:27:11"}]},{"id":"kennedy-enclosure","title":"Requesting a fuller enclosure comparison","meeting":null,"time":null,"topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"The February 2020 minutes record Kennedy’s request for cost information on an enclosure option.","earlier":"The first design cycle was developing a permanent barrier after the Council’s 2018 policy direction.","response":"Staff and the design consultant were asked to return with a fuller comparison. The written minutes are the evidence for this request.","outcome":"The August 2021 presentation included a refined enclosure concept and a design-goal comparison. This is an example of reconsideration producing additional design information.","links":[{"label":"Preserved February 2020 minutes · pp. 2–3","source":"dropbox"},{"label":"Later design-goal comparison","source":"v2021","time":"00:48:33"}],"refs":"10–11","kind":"Minutes summary","basis":"Inherited reading of the City Clerk-supplied February 3, 2020 minutes, pages 2–3, retained in Source 10. No recording was recovered. The Dropbox link opens the preserved-record folder, not a specific PDF.","sortDate":"2020-02-03","date":"February 3, 2020","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"maloyan":{"name":"Ara Maloyan","role":"Public Works director","remarks":[{"id":"maloyan-review","title":"Presenting the task-force alternatives review","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:01:16","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Maloyan presented the task-force recommendations following its comparison of physical barriers, netting, planting, patrols, technology, and self-help measures.","earlier":"The group had gathered public suggestions and compared options using safety, appearance, environmental, rescue, and implementation considerations.","response":"Its recommendation centered on restricting access at the bridge edge while allowing complementary measures.","outcome":"The Council adopted the recommendation later in the meeting. That policy direction still required a detailed design.","links":[{"label":"Task-force report","source":"r2018"},{"label":"Alternatives presentation","source":"p2018"},{"label":"Adopted motion","source":"m2018","page":5}],"refs":"5–7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"maloyan-landscape","title":"Explaining the limits on planting below the bridge","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:04:17","topic":"landscaping","quote":null,"context":"The Public Works presentation identified roughly 20 percent of the span where trees could not be planted.","earlier":"The task-force report had found no verifying data for the proposed cushioning or deterrence role and had considered the time needed for trees to mature.","response":"The written recommendation treated planting as a possible supplement to a vertical barrier.","outcome":"Later landscape questions should be read against these limits and against the actual later proposal, rather than treated as identical ideas.","links":[{"label":"Landscaping findings","source":"r2018","page":3},{"label":"Later Madison question","source":"v2023","time":"00:33:24"},{"label":"Later Delgado suggestions","source":"vjan24","time":"00:47:03"}],"refs":"5, 7, 14, 33","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"maloyan-consultant-scope","title":"Explaining what the next consultant would design","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:20:58","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"In response to McAustin, Maloyan said the next phase would develop vertical-barrier options.","earlier":"The task force had compared the types of intervention. The next question concerned the design within the recommended approach.","response":"At 01:23:11 he explained that several options would return to the community for review.","outcome":"The Council approved the policy direction, with design development, environmental work, and further public review still ahead.","links":[{"label":"McAustin’s scope question","source":"v2018","time":"01:20:21"},{"label":"Range-of-options answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:23:11"},{"label":"Next-phase tasks","source":"r2018","page":3}],"refs":"5–7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"}]},"tornek":{"name":"Terry Tornek","role":"Mayor","remarks":[{"id":"tornek-consensus","title":"Anticipating a harder design debate","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:36:06","topic":"design","quote":"This is probably the highest level of consensus we’ll achieve in this process, I predict.","context":"Tornek predicted that agreement would become more difficult once specific designs were presented.","earlier":"The Council had heard the task-force recommendation, questions about effectiveness, and the scope of the next design phase.","response":"His observation distinguished agreement on a safety approach from agreement on what a permanent structure should look like.","outcome":"The Council nevertheless adopted the task-force direction unanimously. Years of design consideration followed.","links":[{"label":"Council minutes","source":"m2018","page":5},{"label":"Later “harbinger” remark","source":"v2018","time":"01:39:13"}],"refs":"6–7","kind":"Retained quotation","basis":"Quotation retained in the sent account and preserved April 2018 working transcript. The official-player locator is retained in Source 7. No new listening was performed.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"tornek-urgency","title":"Asking for a shorter consultant schedule","meeting":null,"time":null,"topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"The April 2019 minutes record Tornek’s concern that the proposed schedule was too slow given the urgency of the issue.","earlier":"The City had set aside design money in 2018 and completed a consultant solicitation and interviews.","response":"Madison cautioned about rushing a historic-landmark project but supported a revised schedule, budget review, and grant search.","outcome":"In May, staff returned with a ten-month reduction in the design schedule and a lower proposed consultant contract. Tornek seconded the revised recommendation on May 15.","links":[{"label":"Preserved April and May 2019 minutes · pp. 2–3","source":"dropbox"},{"label":"Revised contract report","source":"r2019","page":2},{"label":"Revised schedule presentation","source":"v2019","time":"00:50:01"}],"refs":"8","kind":"Minutes summary","basis":"Inherited original-minutes reading retained in Source 8. No recording is available in the reviewed record for the April 17 or May 15 committee exchanges. The Dropbox link opens the preserved-record folder.","sortDate":"2019-04-17","date":"April 17, 2019","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"wilson":{"name":"Andy Wilson","role":"Councilmember","remarks":[{"id":"wilson-substitution","title":"Asking whether deaths would move elsewhere","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:24:11","topic":"effectiveness","quote":null,"context":"Wilson asked whether restricting access at this bridge would simply move the problem to another location.","earlier":"The task-force recommendation concerned access restriction at the bridge. Effects at nearby sites and across a region are separate outcomes.","response":"Mermell joined the discussion at 01:25:58. Wilson called on Public Health at 01:26:46, and Michael Johnson began answering at 01:27:14.","outcome":"Johnson described evidence of broader reductions. This records the public answer given in 2018, not a new review establishing the same outcome in every setting.","links":[{"label":"Public Health answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"},{"label":"Gordo’s regional follow-up","source":"v2018","time":"01:31:15"},{"label":"Johnson’s Toronto reference","source":"v2018","time":"01:32:15"}],"refs":"7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"},{"id":"wilson-timetable","title":"Calling attention to the length of the project","meeting":"v2019","time":"00:51:54","topic":"design","quote":"almost a four or five year project","context":"Wilson described the undertaking as “almost a four or five year project,” even after staff shortened the proposed design schedule.","earlier":"The April committee had asked staff to revisit the schedule and budget.","response":"Staff reported a ten-month shorter design period, a contract reduction from $700,000 to $500,000, and an anticipated August 2020 construction start.","outcome":"The Council approved the consultant contract. That forecast was not a funded construction deadline or evidence that construction actually began in August 2020.","links":[{"label":"Staff report","source":"r2019","page":2},{"label":"Schedule and contract changes","source":"v2019","time":"00:50:01"},{"label":"Construction forecast","source":"v2019","time":"00:51:05"}],"refs":"8","kind":"Retained quotation","basis":"Short quotation and approximate recording locator retained in Source 8. The written report supports the schedule and contract changes. No new listening was performed.","sortDate":"2019-05-20","date":"May 20, 2019","body":"City Council"}]},"mcaustin":{"name":"Margaret McAustin","role":"Councilmember","remarks":[{"id":"mcaustin-scope","title":"Asking what the consultant would actually study","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:20:21","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"McAustin asked what work the design consultant would undertake, then asked at 01:22:55 whether a range of options would be developed.","earlier":"The task force had already compared intervention types and recommended a vertical barrier with end treatments.","response":"Maloyan answered at 01:20:58 that the next phase concerned vertical barriers and at 01:23:11 that different designs would return for community review.","outcome":"This exchange records the intended distinction between choosing the safety approach and developing its design.","links":[{"label":"Maloyan’s scope answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:20:58"},{"label":"McAustin’s options follow-up","source":"v2018","time":"01:22:55"},{"label":"Maloyan’s options answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:23:11"}],"refs":"7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"}]},"markarian":{"name":"Kris Markarian","role":"Public Works","remarks":[{"id":"markarian-procurement","title":"Explaining a pandemic procurement and fabrication delay","meeting":"v2021","time":"00:14:14","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Markarian described delays obtaining materials and fabricating the mockups during the pandemic.","earlier":"The project had been through design development and was using full-scale mockups for review.","response":"The explanation concerns procurement and fabrication. It does not independently establish that all design work stopped.","outcome":"The committee reviewed the design results in August 2021 and sought additional work.","links":[{"label":"August 2021 staff report","source":"r2021"}],"refs":"24","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"The author confirmed the highlighted 00:14:14–00:14:32 passage against the official player. This summary preserves the procurement and fabrication scope of that check. No fresh listening was performed.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"markarian-staffing","title":"Explaining why staffing had not been recommended as a replacement","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:47:01","topic":"staffing","quote":null,"context":"Markarian said the task force had considered staffing and described the problem of continuous coverage, the bridge’s curve, multiple personnel, liability, and incidents outside ordinary hours.","earlier":"Patrol and active intervention had been part of the original alternatives review. Mermell had also addressed a staffing proposal in August 2021.","response":"Officials continued asking about trained security and a daytime human presence. The distinction between complementing a barrier and replacing it remained important.","outcome":"The committee requested more staffing analysis. Staff presented annual cost estimates in July 2024.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Mermell response","source":"v2021","time":"01:27:11"},{"label":"Gordo’s further question","source":"v2023","time":"00:51:52"},{"label":"July 2024 presentation","source":"p2024"}],"refs":"11, 15–16","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"markarian-buys-time","title":"Confirming that a barrier creates time for intervention","meeting":"v2023","time":"01:01:26","topic":"effectiveness","quote":"Yes, it buys time","context":"Markarian agreed that a barrier buys time during an incident.","earlier":"Madison had questioned how quickly personnel could reach someone and how staffing would work without a barrier.","response":"This answer describes an opportunity for intervention created by restricting access. It does not isolate the effect of staffing or establish staffing as a substitute.","outcome":"The subsequent analysis continued to consider staffing costs and physical protection separately.","links":[{"label":"Madison’s preceding question","source":"v2023","time":"00:52:29"},{"label":"July 2024 staffing analysis","source":"p2024"}],"refs":"15","kind":"Author-checked quotation","basis":"Phrase and approximate player locator retained in the author-checked November 2023 passage. This website did not perform new listening.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"markarian-structural","title":"Identifying unfinished engineering questions about netting","meeting":"v2024","time":"00:27:24","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Markarian explained that structural connections had not been designed and that further studies were needed to determine whether modifications to the bridge would be required.","earlier":"Staff had presented preliminary engineering and local constraints for a horizontal net.","response":"These were limits of the preliminary assessment. They are not proof that a finished net design had established impossibility.","outcome":"Staff still recommended against netting after considering engineering, rescue, and other factors.","links":[{"label":"Engineering presentation","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Fire’s rescue assessment","source":"v2024","time":"00:34:00"}],"refs":"16–18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"The author confirmed the highlighted 00:27:24–00:27:57 passage against the official recording. This summary preserves the unfinished connections and further-study qualification. No new listening was performed.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"markarian-netting-recommendation","title":"Reporting the expert recommendation against a local net","meeting":"v2024","time":"00:51:03","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Markarian reported a unanimous expert-review recommendation against horizontal netting as a viable measure at this bridge.","earlier":"The presentation had covered local structure, bridge geometry, rescue access, and consultation about Golden Gate.","response":"That recommendation is distinct from the committee’s authority to choose a design and from a general finding about whether nets can work elsewhere.","outcome":"Jones later asked whether any expert had recommended netting. Staff again described the recommendation as unanimous.","links":[{"label":"Staff presentation","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Jones’s later question","source":"v2024","time":"01:18:41"}],"refs":"16, 18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"markarian-exhausted","title":"Responding when below-bridge alternatives returned","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:06:45","topic":"landscaping","quote":"we have truly exhausted various mitigation measures","context":"Madison asked at 01:06:35 whether the City had exhausted all the feasible options. Markarian’s answer began at 01:06:45. She described more than seven years on the project and experts from different backgrounds and perspectives, then discussed the Golden Gate comparison.","earlier":"The earlier task-force report had assessed landscaping. The current presentation had also returned to netting, rescue, staffing, and technology.","response":"Her answer emphasized the work already performed. The continuing discussion still included questions about particular below-bridge arrangements.","outcome":"No permanent design was selected at this meeting. The statement does not prove every possible intervention had been tested.","links":[{"label":"Madison’s question about feasible options","source":"v2024","time":"01:06:35"},{"label":"Leadership prelude and context","source":"v2024","time":"01:06:24"},{"label":"2018 landscaping findings","source":"r2018","page":3},{"label":"July presentation","source":"p2024"}],"refs":"5, 18","kind":"Author-checked quotation","basis":"The author manually checked the official recording on September 22, 2026, confirming the quoted wording and the exchange locators. The displayed 01:06:45 marks the start of Markarian’s answer, not the exact instant of the quoted phrase. Her introductory account and the Golden Gate discussion are summarized. This check does not cover the whole meeting.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"mermell":{"name":"Steve Mermell","role":"City manager","remarks":[{"id":"mermell-opening","title":"Requesting discussion and direction on the designs","meeting":"v2021","time":"00:07:39","topic":"design","quote":"get it right","context":"Mermell opened the item by seeking committee discussion and direction on the permanent designs.","earlier":"Staff had completed substantial design work, commission review, and full-scale mockups.","response":"His “get it right” phrase introduced the request for direction. It was not itself a finding that another round of work was redundant.","outcome":"The committee did not select a final design and requested further consideration.","links":[{"label":"Staff report and alternatives","source":"r2021"},{"label":"Closing summary","source":"v2021","time":"01:43:48"}],"refs":"9, 11","kind":"Retained quotation","basis":"Short phrase and approximate player locator retained in Source 11. The opening passage was also consulted in the preserved captions. No fresh listening was performed.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"mermell-staffing","title":"Responding to the proposal for around-the-clock staffing","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:27:11","topic":"staffing","quote":null,"context":"Mermell said staffing had been suggested before, would likely cost several hundred thousand dollars annually, and was not feasible as a replacement for physical protection.","earlier":"Hampton had proposed staffing the bridge 24 hours a day at 01:26:44.","response":"The discussion continued with Gordo’s Host/Guide-style idea and questions about how staff could intervene.","outcome":"Further examination of staffing alongside a barrier remained in the discussion. The permanent design remained unresolved.","links":[{"label":"Hampton’s proposal","source":"v2021","time":"01:26:44"},{"label":"Later coverage explanation","source":"v2023","time":"00:47:01"}],"refs":"11, 15","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"mermell-return","title":"Explaining why a three-month return was too aggressive","meeting":"v2021","time":"01:43:48","topic":"design","quote":"I do think three months is probably too aggressive.","context":"In his closing summary, Mermell described the further work and said the proposed three-month timetable was probably too aggressive.","earlier":"Committee members had asked for more design work, additional expertise, and further consideration of staffing.","response":"He connected the return schedule to the work now being requested. The temporary fence would remain during that process.","outcome":"This records staff’s stated timing concern, not a calculation of how much delay any individual caused.","links":[{"label":"Committee record","source":"m2021"},{"label":"August staff report","source":"r2021"}],"refs":"11","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The time is an approximate passage locator. Caption cue starts can differ from the retained player locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"olmos":{"name":"Tony Olmos","role":"Public Works director","remarks":[{"id":"olmos-next-steps","title":"Setting out the work for the next return","meeting":"v2023","time":"01:02:09","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Olmos proposed continuing the planned meetings and returning with survey results, responses to the committee’s questions, and Public Health and other expert input.","earlier":"The committee had discussed the three designs, previously studied alternatives, staffing, and the interpretation of suicide-prevention research.","response":"Jones had supported continuing the commission reviews and survey. Olmos’s closing proposal collected those next steps into a return plan.","outcome":"The record shows a stated plan for further work. It does not establish that a permanent design was approved or that all later milestones were completed.","links":[{"label":"Question about continuing or pausing","source":"v2023","time":"00:54:37"},{"label":"Jones’s response","source":"v2023","time":"00:55:35"},{"label":"Planned next steps","source":"p2023","page":34}],"refs":"6, 14","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"The author manually checked the speaker and approximate player locator for the 01:02:09 closing proposal, as retained in Source 14. No new listening was performed.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"olmos-pause","title":"Asking whether staff should continue or pause","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:54:37","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Olmos asked whether staff should continue through the scheduled commission reviews and survey or pause the process.","earlier":"The committee had raised further questions about alternatives and staffing while three new designs were being considered.","response":"Jones recommended continuing to the commissions and hearing the survey results at 00:55:35.","outcome":"Commission review and the survey proceeded. This exchange records a concrete request for direction rather than an absence of staff work.","links":[{"label":"Jones’s response","source":"v2023","time":"00:55:35"},{"label":"2023 presentation","source":"p2023"}],"refs":"14","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"The author manually checked this November 2023 speaker handoff and approximate player locator, as retained in Source 14. This website presents a summary and performed no new listening.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"olmos-health","title":"Agreeing to bring Public Health into the next review","meeting":"v2023","time":"00:57:36","topic":"effectiveness","quote":null,"context":"Olmos responded to questions about Public Health involvement and whether protection at one location moves deaths elsewhere.","earlier":"Public Health had discussed regional effects and substitution with the Council in April 2018.","response":"Staff agreed to include health professionals when the project returned. Earlier research did not eliminate the need to state its limits or explain it to the current discussion.","outcome":"A suicide prevention specialist participated in the July 2024 presentation.","links":[{"label":"2018 Public Health answer","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"},{"label":"Jones’s question","source":"v2023","time":"00:56:47"},{"label":"July education discussion","source":"v2024","time":"00:42:41"}],"refs":"7, 14, 19","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"The author manually checked this November 2023 speaker handoff and approximate player locator, as retained in Source 14. This website presents a summary and performed no new listening.","sortDate":"2023-11-15","date":"November 15, 2023","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"johnson":{"name":"Michael Johnson","role":"Public Health director","remarks":[{"id":"johnson-regional","title":"Answering the substitution and regional-effect questions","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:27:14","topic":"effectiveness","quote":null,"context":"Johnson described research suggesting that barriers at bridges known for suicide could reduce broader deaths rather than merely shift them elsewhere.","earlier":"Wilson had asked about substitution. Gordo followed with a regional-effect question at 01:31:15.","response":"Johnson answered the follow-up at 01:31:51 and referred to Toronto research at 01:32:15.","outcome":"This establishes what the Council was told in 2018. Different sites, follow-up periods, regional jumping deaths, and all-method deaths must remain distinct when evaluating the original research.","links":[{"label":"Wilson’s question","source":"v2018","time":"01:24:11"},{"label":"Gordo’s follow-up","source":"v2018","time":"01:31:15"},{"label":"Johnson’s study reference","source":"v2018","time":"01:32:15"}],"refs":"7, 29–30","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"}]},"line":{"name":"Steve Line","role":"Design consultant","remarks":[{"id":"line-comparison","title":"Comparing the refined enclosure against design goals","meeting":"v2021","time":"00:48:33","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Line compared the developed concepts, including a refined enclosure, against the project’s design goals.","earlier":"Kennedy had requested a fuller enclosure cost comparison in February 2020. The design team subsequently developed more detail.","response":"The presentation compared safety, appearance, and the relationship to the historic bridge. A concept comparison was not final design approval.","outcome":"Kennedy later supported narrowing to vertical-barrier options. This sequence preserves an instance in which reconsideration added design information.","links":[{"label":"Preserved February 2020 minutes","source":"dropbox"},{"label":"August 2021 report","source":"r2021"},{"label":"Kennedy on narrowing","source":"v2021","time":"01:16:41"}],"refs":"10–11","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2021-08-18","date":"August 18, 2021","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"augustin":{"name":"Chad Augustin","role":"Fire chief","remarks":[{"id":"augustin-rescue","title":"Explaining the rescue concerns with horizontal netting","meeting":"v2024","time":"00:34:00","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Augustin discussed the safety of the person in crisis and responding crews, access to a net, the use of two ladder trucks, extended operations, and training requirements.","earlier":"The City had consulted about netting and developed a preliminary local engineering assessment.","response":"At approximately 00:35:48, he said he could not recommend horizontal netting from a public-safety standpoint.","outcome":"The recommendation was about local rescue operations. It does not establish that nets are universally ineffective or that all engineering questions had been resolved.","links":[{"label":"Fire’s recommendation","source":"v2024","time":"00:35:48"},{"label":"Staff presentation","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Unfinished structural questions","source":"v2024","time":"00:27:24"}],"refs":"16–18","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"augustin-vertical","title":"Clarifying support for a vertical barrier","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:09:16","topic":"netting","quote":"we absolutely are in support of a barrier","context":"Augustin clarified that Fire’s concern was with horizontal netting and that the department supported a vertical barrier.","earlier":"Gordo’s preceding question had raised the netting and rescue issue. Staff had already explained the local rescue difficulties.","response":"The clarification prevents the recommendation against netting from being read as opposition to physical protection generally.","outcome":"The committee continued discussing the options without selecting a permanent design.","links":[{"label":"Earlier Fire explanation","source":"v2024","time":"00:34:00"},{"label":"Committee minutes","source":"m2024"}],"refs":"18, 22","kind":"Caption excerpt","basis":"Excerpt read in the preserved City-hosted captions, with punctuation and capitalization adjusted for reading. The time is an approximate passage locator. Caption cue starts can differ from the retained player locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"},{"id":"augustin-cushion","title":"Describing the rescue cushion’s operational limits","meeting":"v2024","time":"01:20:04","topic":"netting","quote":null,"context":"Augustin described a roughly 100-foot cushion rating, bridge portions reaching roughly 150 feet, and constraints on positioning equipment below the bridge.","earlier":"Hampton had returned to below-bridge measures after the netting and rescue presentation.","response":"Hampton then asked whether higher-capacity equipment existed. Fire did not know and agreed to investigate.","outcome":"The reviewed record does not establish that a higher-capacity cushion was acquired. The May 2026 Fire equipment list names a cushion but supplies no rating.","links":[{"label":"Higher-capacity question","source":"v2024","time":"01:20:55"},{"label":"May 2026 equipment list · packet p. 22","source":"fire2026"}],"refs":"21","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Summary follows the retained Source 21 locators and local operating limits. The May 2026 equipment-list reference is a separate documentary check, not proof that the 2024 question was answered. No new listening was performed.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"kramer":{"name":"Sandri Kramer","role":"Suicide prevention specialist","remarks":[{"id":"kramer-education","title":"Explaining the need for public education about prevention","meeting":"v2024","time":"00:42:41","topic":"effectiveness","quote":"public education issue","context":"Kramer described a public education issue in understanding suicide prevention and the role of barriers in creating time for intervention.","earlier":"The survey had shown uncertainty about both the need for a vertical barrier and whether it would work. The responses were public opinion, not a clinical-outcome study.","response":"Her discussion placed intervention time and the impact on responders alongside the project’s engineering and design questions.","outcome":"This supports reading the survey alongside research and professional explanation. It does not turn survey preferences into evidence of effectiveness.","links":[{"label":"Survey presentation","source":"p2024"},{"label":"Earlier Public Health discussion","source":"v2018","time":"01:27:14"}],"refs":"19","kind":"Retained quotation","basis":"Short phrase and player locator retained in Source 19. Selected preserved captions were consulted. This was not a new full-presentation or audio verification.","sortDate":"2024-07-17","date":"July 17, 2024","body":"Public Safety Committee"}]},"mossman":{"name":"Sue Mossman","role":"Pasadena Heritage","remarks":[{"id":"mossman-preservation","title":"Describing the preservation and safety tradeoff","meeting":null,"time":null,"topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"In a 2018 interview, Mossman discussed the difficulty of protecting people while respecting the bridge’s historic design.","earlier":"The Council had adopted the task-force recommendation, with the permanent design still to be developed.","response":"Her comments described a preservation perspective. They were not a clinical finding or a City decision.","outcome":"The 2021 committee minutes later record her appreciation for the staff and design team, the lack of task-force consensus on the designs, and her call for further exploration.","links":[{"label":"Los Angeles magazine interview","source":"lamag"},{"label":"August 2021 minutes","source":"m2021","page":2},{"label":"Winter 2026 preservation commentary · p. 8","source":"wpra"}],"refs":"23","kind":"Interview summary","basis":"The June 18, 2018 Los Angeles magazine interview was read as text. The later August 2021 minutes supply documentary context, not an inferred timestamp. The Winter 2026 column is a separately retained source. No recorded utterance is claimed here.","sortDate":"2018-06-18","date":"June 18, 2018","body":"Published interview"}]},"masuda":{"name":"Gene Masuda","role":"Councilmember","remarks":[{"id":"masuda-access","title":"Asking whether pedestrian access could be prohibited","meeting":"v2018","time":"01:14:36","topic":"design","quote":null,"context":"Masuda asked whether pedestrians could simply be prohibited from the bridge.","earlier":"The task force was recommending a physical barrier that would allow the bridge to continue serving its public uses.","response":"Maloyan answered that a pedestrian ban would not prevent access without closing the bridge to vehicle traffic as well, which he called impossible.","outcome":"The Council proceeded with the barrier recommendation. This was a transportation and access explanation, not a study measuring the effects of a pedestrian ban.","links":[{"label":"Task-force report","source":"r2018"},{"label":"Council minutes","source":"m2018"}],"refs":"7","kind":"Discussion summary","basis":"Discussion summary carried from the retained source note and supporting record. The time is an approximate official-player passage locator. No new listening was performed for this website.","sortDate":"2018-04-23","date":"April 23, 2018","body":"City Council"}]}};
